1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,850
Chapter 67: Infectious Diseases.

3
00:00:05,850 --> 00:00:18,950
Maternal And Fetal Immunology Viral Infections Bacterial Infections Protozoal Infections Bioterrorism References Infections remain a major cause of maternal and fetal morbidity and mortality worldwide.

4
00:00:18,950 --> 00:00:29,725
The unique maternal–fetal vascular interface in some cases serves to protect the fetus from infectious agents, but in other instances it provides a conduit for transmission.

5
00:00:29,725 --> 00:00:40,800
Maternal serological status, acquisition mode, gestational age at the time of infection, and immunological status of mother and fetus all influence disease outcome.

6
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:49,862
The TORCH acronym reflects the infections toxoplasmosis, others, rubella, and cytomegalovirus and herpesvirus infections.

7
00:00:49,862 --> 00:00:55,737
These can cross the placenta to cause fetal infection and substantial neonatal sequelae.

8
00:00:55,737 --> 00:01:01,712
Of members in the “others” category, parvovirus B19 infection is discussed here.

9
00:01:01,712 --> 00:01:06,837
Herpesvirus and syphilis infections are described in Chapter 68.

10
00:01:06,837 --> 00:01:09,625
Maternal And Fetal Immunology.

11
00:01:09,625 --> 00:01:13,075
# Maternal Immunological Changes.

12
00:01:13,075 --> 00:01:20,525
Despite significant advances, many of the maternal immunological adaptations to pregnancy remain unclear.

13
00:01:20,525 --> 00:01:28,562
Successful pregnancy results from a complex interplay between the innate and adaptive immune systems to establish fetal tolerance .

14
00:01:28,562 --> 00:01:43,700
Within the innate system, decidual natural killer (dNK) cells interact with and aid invasion of fetal extravillous trophoblasts via human leukocyte antigen (HLA)-G (Fig. 21-8, p. 404).

15
00:01:43,700 --> 00:01:51,462
Also, cytokines produced during viral infection activate dNK cells to induce protective cytotoxicity .

16
00:01:51,462 --> 00:02:03,062
Within the adaptive immune system, a CD4+ subset of T helper cells called regulatory T cells (Tregs) express forkhead box P3 (FOXP3).

17
00:02:03,062 --> 00:02:10,600
This is an immune-suppressive transcription factor that plays a critical role in inducing maternal immune tolerance to the fetus .

18
00:02:10,600 --> 00:02:16,750
However, this same mechanism compromises maternal defense against bacterial pathogens.

19
00:02:16,750 --> 00:02:26,412
Noncoding microRNAs, whose roles are incompletely defined, regulate activation and proliferation of both innate and adaptive immune cells .

20
00:02:26,412 --> 00:02:33,950
In describing infections, horizontal transmission is the spread of an infectious agent from one individual to another.

21
00:02:33,950 --> 00:02:43,075
Vertical transmission is passage of an infectious agent from the mother to her fetus through the placenta, during labor or delivery, or by breastfeeding.

22
00:02:43,075 --> 00:02:50,137
Thus, preterm rupture of membranes or prolonged labor may enhance the risk of some neonatal infections .

23
00:02:50,137 --> 00:02:52,737
Table 67-1.

24
00:02:52,737 --> 00:02:56,937
shows specific infections by mode and acquisition timing.

25
00:02:56,937 --> 00:03:06,925
A final term, the secondary attack rate, is the probability that infection develops in a susceptible individual following known contact with an infectious person.

26
00:03:06,925 --> 00:03:09,525
TABLE 67-1.

27
00:03:09,525 --> 00:03:15,987
Specific Causes of Some Fetal and Neonatal Infections # Fetal and Newborn Immunology.

28
00:03:15,987 --> 00:03:23,412
The active immunological capacity of the fetus and neonate is compromised compared with that of older children and adults.

29
00:03:23,412 --> 00:03:29,975
That said, fetal innate and adaptive immunity begin to develop by 9 to 15 weeks’ gestation .

30
00:03:29,975 --> 00:03:41,450
The primary fetal response to infection is mediated by innate immunity—macrophages and dendritic cells—and to a lesser extent, by adaptive immunity—B and T cells .

31
00:03:41,450 --> 00:03:48,650
Passive immunity is provided by maternal immunoglobulin G (IgG) transferred across the placenta.

32
00:03:48,650 --> 00:03:58,375
By 16 weeks’ gestation, this transfer begins to rise rapidly, and by 26 weeks, fetal concentrations are equivalent to those of the mother.

33
00:03:58,375 --> 00:04:06,425
Evidence suggests maternal vaccination also primes the fetal cell-mediated immune response independent of passive immunity .

34
00:04:06,425 --> 00:04:16,462
Neonatal infection, especially in its early stages, may be difficult to diagnose because these newborns often fail to express classic clinical signs.

35
00:04:16,462 --> 00:04:23,300
If the fetus was infected in utero, it may be depressed and acidotic at birth for no apparent reason.

36
00:04:23,300 --> 00:04:28,137
The neonate may suck poorly, vomit, or show abdominal distention.

37
00:04:28,137 --> 00:04:35,525
Respiratory insufficiency can develop, which may present similarly to idiopathic respiratory distress syndrome.

38
00:04:35,525 --> 00:04:38,675
The neonate may be lethargic or jittery.

39
00:04:38,675 --> 00:04:46,425
The response to sepsis may be hypothermia rather than hyperthermia, and the total leukocyte and neutrophil counts can be

40
00:04:46,588 --> 00:04:48,213
depressed.

41
00:04:48,163 --> 00:04:50,325
Viral Infections.

42
00:04:50,325 --> 00:04:52,975
# Cytomegalovirus.

43
00:04:52,975 --> 00:04:59,538
The most common perinatal infection in the developed world is caused by cytomegalovirus (CMV).

44
00:04:59,538 --> 00:05:10,950
It is a ubiquitous DNA herpesvirus that eventually infects most humans, and some evidence of fetal infection is found in 0.2 to 2.2 percent of all neonates .

45
00:05:10,950 --> 00:05:23,500
The virus is secreted into all body fluids, and person-to-person contact with viral-laden saliva, semen, urine, blood, and nasopharyngeal and cervical secretions can transmit infection.

46
00:05:23,500 --> 00:05:32,063
The fetus may become infected by transplacental viremia, but amniocentesis does not result in iatrogenic fetal transmission .

47
00:05:32,063 --> 00:05:38,100
The neonate is infected at delivery or during breastfeeding and acquisition continues to accrue.

48
00:05:38,100 --> 00:05:42,225
Daycare centers, for example, are a frequent source.

49
00:05:42,225 --> 00:05:55,188
Up to 85 percent of women from lower socioeconomic backgrounds are seropositive by the time of pregnancy, whereas only half of women in higher income groups have detectable IgG antibodies to CMV.

50
00:05:55,188 --> 00:06:07,575
Following primary CMV infection, and behaving similarly to other herpesvirus infections, the virus becomes latent but undergoes periodic reactivation characterized by viral shedding.

51
00:06:07,575 --> 00:06:13,188
This occurs despite high serum levels of anti-CMV IgG antibody.

52
00:06:13,188 --> 00:06:22,838
These antibodies do not prevent maternal recurrence, reactivation, or reinfection, nor do they totally mitigate fetal or neonatal infection.

53
00:06:22,838 --> 00:06:29,288
Maternal Infection Pregnancy does not increase the risk or severity of maternal CMV infection.

54
00:06:29,288 --> 00:06:42,663
Most primary infections are asymptomatic, but 10 to 15 percent of infected adults have a mononucleosis-like syndrome characterized by fever, pharyngitis, lymphadenopathy, and polyarthritis.

55
00:06:42,663 --> 00:06:52,463
Immunocompromised women may develop myocarditis, pneumonitis, hepatitis, retinitis, gastroenteritis, or meningoencephalitis.

56
00:06:52,463 --> 00:06:59,538
Most women with primary infection have elevated serum aminotransferase levels or lymphocytosis.

57
00:06:59,538 --> 00:07:05,138
Reactivated disease usually is asymptomatic, although viral shedding is common.

58
00:07:05,138 --> 00:07:12,588
Because most primary maternal infections are clinically silent, they are detected by IgG seroconversion.

59
00:07:12,588 --> 00:07:17,238
The rate of seroconversion during pregnancy may reach 20 percent.

60
00:07:17,238 --> 00:07:21,100
Rates are higher in regions with greater seroprevalence .

61
00:07:21,100 --> 00:07:31,238
Women who are seronegative before pregnancy and then acquire primary CMV infection during pregnancy are at highest risk of having a congenitally infected fetus.

62
00:07:31,238 --> 00:07:49,463
Vertical transmission rates for primary infection are 30 to 36 percent in the first trimester, 34 to 40 percent in the second, and 40 to 72 percent in the third (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2020a; Picone, 2017).

63
00:07:49,463 --> 00:07:57,463
Approximately 23 percent of congenital CMV infections in the United States are from primary maternal infection .

64
00:07:57,463 --> 00:08:06,613
In contrast, recurrent or reactivated maternal infection infects the fetus in only 0.15 to 2 percent of pregnancies.

65
00:08:06,613 --> 00:08:14,925
However, up to 90 percent of congenitally infected neonates in high-prevalence areas are born to women with recurrent infection .

66
00:08:14,925 --> 00:08:21,013
The diagnosis of recurrent or reactivated maternal CMV infection remains a challenge .

67
00:08:21,013 --> 00:08:30,600
Congenital Infection Newborns with apparent sequelae of intrauterine-acquired CMV infection are described as having symptomatic CMV infection.

68
00:08:30,600 --> 00:08:48,975
Congenital CMV infection is a syndrome that may include growth restriction, microcephaly, intracranial calcifications, chorioretinitis, mental and motor delays, sensorineural deficits, hepatosplenomegaly, jaundice, hemolytic anemia, and thrombocytopenic purpura.

69
00:08:48,975 --> 00:08:54,888
An example of periventricular calcifications is shown in Figure 67-1.

70
00:08:54,888 --> 00:09:03,000
Of the estimated 40,000 CMV-infected neonates born each year, only 5 to 10 percent demonstrate this syndrome .

71
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:09,663
Thus, most infected newborns are asymptomatic at birth, but some develop late-onset sequelae.

72
00:09:09,663 --> 00:09:18,025
These may include hearing loss, neurological deficits, chorioretinitis, psychomotor delay, and learning disabilities.

73
00:09:18,025 --> 00:09:25,300
Infections in dichorionic twins most likely are nonconcordant (Egaña-Ugrinovic, 2016).

74
00:09:25,300 --> 00:09:27,900
Figure 67-1.

75
00:09:27,900 --> 00:09:31,175
Congenital cytomegalovirus infection.

76
00:09:31,175 --> 00:09:39,138
A. Transverse view of the fetal head demonstrates ventriculomegaly (LV) and periventricular calcifications.

77
00:09:39,138 --> 00:09:42,425
B. Transverse image of the fetal abdomen

78
00:09:42,580 --> 00:09:46,492
with ascites (asterisk) and echogenic bowel.

79
00:09:46,442 --> 00:09:52,580
Prenatal Diagnosis Routine prenatal CMV serological screening is not recommended.

80
00:09:52,580 --> 00:10:12,592
Pregnant women undergo serological testing for CMV if they present with a mononucleosis-like illness or if congenital infection is suspected based on abnormal sonographic findings (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2020a; Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine, 2016).

81
00:10:12,592 --> 00:10:19,942
If either ultrasound or serological results indicate infection, diagnostic amniocentesis is pursued.

82
00:10:19,942 --> 00:10:22,605
Figure 67-2.

83
00:10:22,605 --> 00:10:29,730
Diagnostic algorithm for evaluation of cytomegalovirus (CMV) infection in pregnancy.

84
00:10:29,730 --> 00:10:38,767
IgG = immunoglobulin G; IgM = immunoglobulin M; PCR = polymerase chain reaction.

85
00:10:38,767 --> 00:10:52,167
Serum levels of CMV IgM alone do not accurately diagnose primary infection because antibodies may persist for more than a year after infection and may rise again following reactivation or reinfection.

86
00:10:52,167 --> 00:11:04,642
To help clarify a primary infection from a past one, serological CMV-specific IgG avidity assays measure the “avidity” or binding strength between IgG antibodies and virus.

87
00:11:04,642 --> 00:11:13,280
Following primary infection, IgG antibodies have low avidity, which during the next 2 to 4 months matures to high avidity.

88
00:11:13,280 --> 00:11:25,292
Low IgG avidity indicates primary CMV infection within the preceding 3 to 4 months, and high IgG avidity excludes primary infection within the previous 3 months.

89
00:11:25,292 --> 00:11:34,555
Thus, primary infection during the first trimester may not be excluded if maternal avidity testing is performed in the second or third trimesters .

90
00:11:34,555 --> 00:11:42,317
In this case, diagnostic amniocentesis is still recommended if ultrasound findings suggest congenital infection.

91
00:11:42,317 --> 00:11:48,942
In other cases, early primary infection may be so recent that IgM is not yet detectable.

92
00:11:48,942 --> 00:11:54,392
If suspicion is high, serological assays are repeated in 2 to 4 weeks.

93
00:11:54,392 --> 00:12:00,155
More commonly, numerous sonographic fetal findings may prompt CMV testing.

94
00:12:00,155 --> 00:12:17,605
Fetal abnormalities associated with CMV infection include microcephaly, ventriculomegaly, and cerebral calcifications; ascites, hepatomegaly, splenomegaly, and hyperechoic bowel; hydrops; early growth restriction; and oligohydramnios.

95
00:12:17,605 --> 00:12:25,942
Findings will prompt maternal serological CMV testing and diagnostic amniocentesis (see Fig. 67-2).

96
00:12:25,942 --> 00:12:35,467
CMV polymerase chain reaction (PCR) testing of amnionic fluid is considered the gold standard for the diagnosis of fetal infection.

97
00:12:35,467 --> 00:12:41,755
Sensitivities range from 70 to 99 percent and depend on amniocentesis timing.

98
00:12:41,755 --> 00:12:50,505
Sensitivity is highest when amniocentesis is performed at least 6 weeks after maternal infection and after 21 weeks’ gestation .

99
00:12:50,505 --> 00:13:02,330
Abnormal sonographic findings seen in combination with positive findings in amnionic fluid samples are predictive of an approximate 75-percent risk of symptomatic congenital infection .

100
00:13:02,330 --> 00:13:12,005
A negative result from amnionic fluid PCR testing does not exclude fetal infection and may need to be repeated if suspicion for fetal infection is high.

101
00:13:12,005 --> 00:13:18,692
Diagnostic viral culture of amnionic fluid is also suitable although sensitivity is somewhat lower.

102
00:13:18,692 --> 00:13:29,305
Isolation of virus from cell culture allows detection of actively replicating virus, and rapid shell vial culture methods enable detection within 24 hours .

103
00:13:29,305 --> 00:13:39,580
Management and Prevention The management of the immunocompetent pregnant woman with primary, reactivated, or recurrent CMV is limited to symptomatic treatment.

104
00:13:39,580 --> 00:13:52,767
If primary CMV infection is diagnosed serologically or if fetal sonographic abnormalities suggest congenital infection, amnionic fluid analysis should be offered (see Fig. 67-2).

105
00:13:52,767 --> 00:13:59,855
Counseling regarding fetal outcome depends on the gestational age during which primary infection is documented.

106
00:13:59,855 --> 00:14:15,705
Lipitz and associates (2013) reported hearing loss or neurodevelopmental delay in 15 to 25 percent of infants following primary maternal infection in the first trimester compared with 2 to 16 percent after second-trimester infection.

107
00:14:15,705 --> 00:14:23,105
Despite the high infection rate with primary infection in the first half of pregnancy, most fetuses develop normally.

108
00:14:23,105 --> 00:14:27,505
That said, pregnancy termination may be an option for some.

109
00:14:27,505 --> 00:14:32,530
Currently, no curative treatments are available for CMV infection.

110
00:14:32,530 --> 00:14:35,605
One study group reported that treating

111
00:14:35,764 --> 00:14:47,964
pregnant women with high-dose oral valacyclovir, 8 g daily, apparently mitigated adverse outcomes for 80 percent of moderately asymptomatic fetuses compared with a historical cohort .

112
00:14:47,914 --> 00:15:03,676
Kimberlin and colleagues (2015) previously showed that oral valganciclovir administered for 6 weeks to neonates with symptomatic central nervous system (CNS) disease prevented hearing deterioration at 6 months and possibly later.

113
00:15:03,676 --> 00:15:15,114
In a recent randomized trial, CMV-specific hyperimmune globulin given to pregnant women with primary infection was ineffective in lowering perinatal CMV infection rates .

114
00:15:15,114 --> 00:15:21,914
For the CMV-affected fetus, a detailed anatomical sonographic evaluation is performed.

115
00:15:21,914 --> 00:15:35,276
Referral to a maternal–fetal medicine specialist is warranted, and serial sonography is indicated to identify poor growth, oligohydramnios, or fetal hydrops, which confers a worse prognosis.

116
00:15:35,276 --> 00:15:43,951
Antenatal surveillance is typically implemented after a shared discussion about the goals of surveillance in a fetus with known congenital infection.

117
00:15:43,951 --> 00:15:54,526
Delivery at term is the goal, although consultation with maternal–fetal medicine and neonatology experts is warranted to determine whether earlier delivery is indicated.

118
00:15:54,526 --> 00:16:02,026
Prevention of congenital infection relies on avoiding maternal primary infection, especially in early pregnancy.

119
00:16:02,026 --> 00:16:11,064
Basic hygiene measures such as hand washing and not sharing food or utensils have been promoted, particularly in women with toddlers in day care .

120
00:16:11,064 --> 00:16:19,151
CMV may be sexually transmitted among infected partners, but no data address the efficacy of preventive strategies.

121
00:16:19,151 --> 00:16:23,664
Several clinical trials of a CMV vaccine are underway .

122
00:16:23,664 --> 00:16:26,701
# Varicella-Zoster Virus.

123
00:16:26,701 --> 00:16:39,664
Maternal Infection Varicella-zoster virus (VZV) is a double-stranded DNA herpesvirus acquired predominantly during childhood, and 90 percent of adults have serological evidence of immunity.

124
00:16:39,664 --> 00:16:47,839
The incidence of varicella infection declined by approximately 95 percent after the introduction of varicella vaccination.

125
00:16:47,839 --> 00:16:51,801
Maternal and fetal varicella rates subsequently dropped .

126
00:16:51,801 --> 00:17:04,064
In the United States between 2003 and 2010, the incidence of maternal varicella among 7.7 million pregnancy admissions was 1.21 cases in 10,000 .

127
00:17:04,064 --> 00:17:13,439
Primary infection—varicella or chickenpox—is transmitted by respiratory droplets or through direct contact with an infected individual.

128
00:17:13,439 --> 00:17:22,976
The incubation period is 10 to 21 days, and a nonimmune woman has a 70- to 90-percent risk of becoming infected after exposure .

129
00:17:22,976 --> 00:17:32,301
Primary varicella has a 1- to 2-day flu-like prodrome, which is followed by pruritic vesicular lesions that crust after 3 to 7 days.

130
00:17:32,301 --> 00:17:38,939
Affected patients are contagious from 1 day before the rash onset until all lesions become crusted.

131
00:17:38,939 --> 00:17:46,614
In adults, mortality risk rises with age and is predominantly due to secondary infection or VZV pneumonia .

132
00:17:46,614 --> 00:17:53,151
Risk factors for severe pneumonitis include immunosuppression and early bacterial coinfection .

133
00:17:53,151 --> 00:18:00,576
Although the incidence was once thought to be higher in gravidas, only 2.5 percent develops VZV pneumonia .

134
00:18:00,576 --> 00:18:06,326
Symptoms of VZV pneumonia usually appear 3 to 5 days into the illness course.

135
00:18:06,326 --> 00:18:12,589
Fever, tachypnea, dry cough, dyspnea, and pleuritic pain are characteristic.

136
00:18:12,589 --> 00:18:16,889
Nodular infiltrates are similar to other viral pneumonias.

137
00:18:16,889 --> 00:18:25,339
Although resolution of pneumonitis parallels that of skin lesions, fever and compromised pulmonary function may persist for weeks.

138
00:18:25,339 --> 00:18:31,826
If primary varicella is reactivated years later, it causes herpes zoster or shingles.

139
00:18:31,826 --> 00:18:37,889
The associated unilateral dermatomal vesicular eruption often produces severe pain.

140
00:18:37,889 --> 00:18:42,364
Zoster does not appear to be more frequent or severe in gravidas.

141
00:18:42,364 --> 00:18:48,014
Congenital varicella syndrome rarely develops in cases of maternal herpes zoster .

142
00:18:48,014 --> 00:18:52,901
Zoster is contagious if there is direct contact with broken blisters.

143
00:18:52,901 --> 00:19:02,289
Fetal and Neonatal Infection In women with primary varicella during the first half of pregnancy, the fetus may develop congenital varicella syndrome.

144
00:19:02,289 --> 00:19:17,439
Some features include chorioretinitis, microphthalmia, cerebral cortical atrophy, growth restriction, hydronephrosis, limb hypoplasia, and cicatricial skin lesions (Ahn, 2016; Auriti, 2009).

145
00:19:17,439 --> 00:19:21,539
Among 1373 pregnant women with primary

146
00:19:21,700 --> 00:19:31,712
varicella infection in one study, only 0.4 percent with a rash before 13 weeks’ gestation had neonates with congenital varicella syndrome .

147
00:19:31,662 --> 00:19:41,375
The highest risk period for vertical transmission was between 13 and 20 weeks, during which 2 percent of exposed fetuses had evidence of infection.

148
00:19:41,375 --> 00:19:48,000
After 20 weeks’ gestation, the researchers found no clinical evidence of congenital varicella.

149
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:50,900
Others described similar findings .

150
00:19:50,900 --> 00:19:59,450
That said, fetuses that do develop congenital varicella in the third trimester may have CNS abnormalities and skin lesions .

151
00:19:59,450 --> 00:20:10,337
If the fetus or neonate is exposed to active infection just before or during delivery, and therefore before maternal antibody has formed, the newborn faces a serious threat.

152
00:20:10,337 --> 00:20:16,787
Attack rates range from 25 to 50 percent, and mortality rates approach 30 percent.

153
00:20:16,787 --> 00:20:23,800
In some instances, neonates develop disseminated visceral and CNS disease, which is commonly fatal.

154
00:20:23,800 --> 00:20:36,662
For this reason, varicella-zoster immune globulin (VariZIG) should be administered to neonates born to mothers who have clinical evidence of varicella 5 days before and up to 2 days after delivery .

155
00:20:36,662 --> 00:20:44,837
Diagnosis Prior maternal varicella is usually confirmed clinically based on reported history of prior illness or vaccination.

156
00:20:44,837 --> 00:20:52,012
Infection is confirmed by PCR-based testing of vesicular fluid or of a crust that is lifted from the skin.

157
00:20:52,012 --> 00:21:02,987
Congenital varicella may be diagnosed using PCR-based testing of amnionic fluid, although a positive result does not correlate well with the development of congenital infection .

158
00:21:02,987 --> 00:21:13,512
A detailed anatomical sonographic evaluation performed at least 5 weeks after maternal infection may disclose abnormalities, but the sensitivity is low .

159
00:21:13,512 --> 00:21:17,250
Management and Prevention Maternal Viral Exposure.

160
00:21:17,250 --> 00:21:24,587
Any patient diagnosed with primary varicella infection or herpes zoster should be isolated from pregnant women.

161
00:21:24,587 --> 00:21:30,800
Several aspects of maternal VZV exposure and infection in pregnancy affect management.

162
00:21:30,800 --> 00:21:39,500
Exposed gravidas with a negative clinical history for prior chickenpox or vaccination should undergo VZV serological testing.

163
00:21:39,500 --> 00:21:44,437
At least 70 percent of these women will be seropositive and thus immune.

164
00:21:44,437 --> 00:21:57,325
Seronegative women should be given intramuscular VariZIG, 125 units per 10 kg body weight, up to a maximum dose of 625 units (5 vials).

165
00:21:57,325 --> 00:22:06,337
Although best given within 96 hours of exposure, its use is approved for up to 10 days to prevent or attenuate varicella infection .

166
00:22:06,337 --> 00:22:10,350
This passive immunization appears to be highly effective .

167
00:22:10,350 --> 00:22:17,587
In women with a reported history of chickenpox or varicella vaccination, VariZIG is not indicated.

168
00:22:17,587 --> 00:22:20,237
Maternal and Fetal Infection.

169
00:22:20,237 --> 00:22:29,200
For women with primary varicella, a chest radiograph is recommended by many because VZV pneumonia often presents with few symptoms.

170
00:22:29,200 --> 00:22:37,850
Most women require only supportive care, but those who require intravenous fluids and especially those with pneumonia are hospitalized.

171
00:22:37,850 --> 00:22:47,700
Acyclovir therapy is given to women requiring hospitalization —10 to 15 mg/kg intravenously every 8 hours until afebrile.

172
00:22:47,700 --> 00:22:56,225
This is followed by oral acyclovir, 800 mg five times daily to complete 7 days and until lesions have crusted.

173
00:22:56,225 --> 00:23:07,750
For uncomplicated primary varicella without hospitalization or for herpes zoster, treatment is oral acyclovir, 800 mg five times daily for 7 days.

174
00:23:07,750 --> 00:23:13,737
For the VZV-affected fetus, greater antenatal surveillance is typically implemented.

175
00:23:13,737 --> 00:23:17,762
Serial sonography may help identify poor growth.

176
00:23:17,762 --> 00:23:22,725
Delivery at term is the norm unless fetal findings prompt earlier timing.

177
00:23:22,725 --> 00:23:24,575
Vaccination.

178
00:23:24,575 --> 00:23:32,250
An attenuated live-virus vaccine is recommended for nonpregnant adolescents and adults with no history of varicella.

179
00:23:32,250 --> 00:23:36,512
Two doses are needed and are separated by 4 to 8 weeks.

180
00:23:36,512 --> 00:23:44,112
The vaccine is not recommended for gravidas or for those who may become pregnant within one month following each vaccine dose.

181
00:23:44,112 --> 00:23:55,225
However, a registry of more than 1000 vaccine-exposed pregnancies reports no cases of congenital varicella syndrome or other associated congenital malformations .

182
00:23:55,225 --> 00:23:59,600
The attenuated vaccine virus is not secreted in breast milk.

183
00:23:59,600 --> 00:24:04,650
Thus, postpartum vaccination should not be delayed because of breastfeeding .

184
00:24:04,650 --> 00:24:07,387
# Influenza Virus.

185
00:24:07,387 --> 00:24:10,150
Influenza A and B belong

186
00:24:10,300 --> 00:24:16,787
to the Orthomyxoviridae family of RNA viruses, and both cause epidemic human disease .

187
00:24:16,737 --> 00:24:25,187
Influenza A viruses are subclassified further by hemagglutinin (H) and neuraminidase (N) surface antigens.

188
00:24:25,187 --> 00:24:34,050
Influenza outbreaks occur annually, and severity is determined by the number of outpatient visits, hospitalizations, and deaths .

189
00:24:34,050 --> 00:24:43,900
Maternal Infection Fever, dry cough, and systemic symptoms characterize this infection, which usually is not life-threatening in otherwise healthy adults.

190
00:24:43,900 --> 00:24:55,787
However, pregnant women in general are more susceptible to serious complications, particularly pulmonary involvement (Mertz, 2017; Rasmussen, 2012).

191
00:24:55,787 --> 00:25:05,925
Severe infection during the 2009 to 2010 pandemic had a maternal mortality rate of 1 percent and caused 12 percent of pregnancy-related deaths .

192
00:25:05,925 --> 00:25:11,437
No firm evidence links influenza A virus and congenital malformations.

193
00:25:11,437 --> 00:25:19,875
Rather, the increased rates of neural-tube defects seen in neonates born to women with influenza may be associated with hyperthermia.

194
00:25:19,875 --> 00:25:24,450
Viremia is infrequent, and transplacental passage is rare .

195
00:25:24,450 --> 00:25:39,462
Stillbirth, preterm delivery, and first-trimester abortion have all been reported, but these usually correlate with maternal infection severity (Fell, 2017; Meijer, 2015; Wang, 2021).

196
00:25:39,462 --> 00:25:51,400
Influenza may be detected by nasopharyngeal swab of symptomatic patients using rapid molecular or viral antigen detection assays known as rapid influenza diagnostic tests (RIDTs).

197
00:25:51,400 --> 00:26:06,037
Recently, commercially available multiplex molecular assays have allowed nucleic acid detection and differentiation of both influenza and severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS)-CoV-2 respiratory viruses.

198
00:26:06,037 --> 00:26:13,237
Molecular tests are more sensitive and specific than RIDTs, which have sensitivities of 50 to 70 percent.

199
00:26:13,237 --> 00:26:23,650
Decisions to administer antiviral medications for influenza treatment or chemoprophylaxis should be based on clinical symptoms and epidemiological factors.

200
00:26:23,650 --> 00:26:28,762
Namely, the start of therapy should not be delayed pending testing results.

201
00:26:28,762 --> 00:26:36,750
Management Of available agents, neuraminidase inhibitors are highly effective for the treatment of early influenza A and B.

202
00:26:36,750 --> 00:26:50,612
These include oseltamivir (Tamiflu), which is taken orally for treatment and for chemoprophylaxis; zanamivir (Relenza), which is inhaled for treatment; and peramivir (Rapivab), which is administered intravenously.

203
00:26:50,612 --> 00:27:03,600
A newer agent, baloxavir marboxil (Xofluza), was approved by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration in 2018 for acute uncomplicated influenza, although data in pregnancy are limited.

204
00:27:03,600 --> 00:27:08,062
In pregnancy, experience is most robust with oseltamivir.

205
00:27:08,062 --> 00:27:28,137
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019a) and the Infectious Diseases Society of America recommend oral oseltamivir—75 mg twice daily for 5 days—for pregnant women with suspected or confirmed influenza, ideally within 48 hours of symptom onset .

206
00:27:28,137 --> 00:27:32,250
Early administration may reduce length of hospital stays .

207
00:27:32,250 --> 00:27:43,250
For prophylaxis following exposure to a contact with confirmed infection, oseltamivir, 75 mg orally once daily for 7 days, is recommended.

208
00:27:43,250 --> 00:27:49,275
Antibacterial medications are added when a secondary bacterial pneumonia is suspected .

209
00:27:49,275 --> 00:28:00,925
Vaccination Effective vaccines against influenza are formulated annually and encouraged for all women who will be pregnant during the influenza season, which extends from October through March.

210
00:28:00,925 --> 00:28:13,975
Vaccination throughout the influenza season, but optimally before the end of October, is recommended (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2019c; Grohskopf, 2020).

211
00:28:13,975 --> 00:28:25,562
This is especially important for those affected by chronic medical disorders such as diabetes, heart disease, asthma, or human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection.

212
00:28:25,562 --> 00:28:34,000
Influenza vaccination was associated with a 40-percent reduction in influenza-associated hospitalizations in pregnant women .

213
00:28:34,000 --> 00:28:45,950
Importantly, no evidence shows teratogenicity or developmental problems in children exposed to maternal vaccination (Conlin, 2018; Ludvigsson, 2016).

214
00:28:45,950 --> 00:28:55,325
Moreover, for mothers vaccinated during pregnancy, their infants are protected from lower respiratory tract infections for the first 3 months of life .

215
00:28:55,325 --> 00:28:59,000
All inactivated or recombinant influenza

216
00:28:59,164 --> 00:29:02,214
vaccines may be used in gravidas .

217
00:29:02,164 --> 00:29:10,089
However, a live attenuated influenza virus vaccine available for intranasal use is not recommended for pregnant women.

218
00:29:10,089 --> 00:29:26,026
Inactivated or recombinant influenza vaccines may be administered concomitantly or sequentially with other vaccines including COVID-19 and tetanus toxoid, reduced diphtheria toxoid, and acellular pertussis (Tdap) vaccines.

219
00:29:26,026 --> 00:29:28,539
# Coronavirus.

220
00:29:28,539 --> 00:29:35,101
These single-stranded RNA viruses are named for the “crown” of surface proteins covering the particles.

221
00:29:35,101 --> 00:29:44,451
Seven coronaviruses infect humans, four of which cause upper respiratory disease characterized by rhinorrhea, sneezing, and congestion .

222
00:29:44,451 --> 00:29:54,001
Three newer coronaviruses of zoonotic origin have emerged in the past two decades and may cause serious lower respiratory tract illness in humans.

223
00:29:54,001 --> 00:30:03,501
These are SARS-CoV, Middle East respiratory syndrome (MERS-CoV), and the novel coronavirus of 2019 (SARS-CoV-2).

224
00:30:03,501 --> 00:30:09,964
The first SARS-CoV emerged from China in 2003 and infected more than 8000 people.

225
00:30:09,964 --> 00:30:17,751
In a cohort of 12 pregnant women with confirmed infection, the predominant symptoms were myalgia, chills, and cough .

226
00:30:17,751 --> 00:30:30,626
All women had radiographic evidence of atypical pneumonia, 33 percent required mechanical ventilation, and 80 percent of women diagnosed after 24 weeks’ gestation delivered preterm.

227
00:30:30,626 --> 00:30:36,739
Although no vertical transmission occurred, the maternal fatality rate was 25 percent.

228
00:30:36,739 --> 00:30:46,051
MERS-CoV emerged in the Middle East in 2012 and can cause respiratory illness, acute kidney injury, and gastrointestinal illness .

229
00:30:46,051 --> 00:30:53,314
Although experience with MERS-CoV in pregnancy is limited, the mortality rate in a small series was 40 percent .

230
00:30:53,314 --> 00:30:56,101
# SARS-CoV-2.

231
00:30:56,101 --> 00:31:03,276
This virus causes the clinical illness known as coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19).

232
00:31:03,276 --> 00:31:16,614
The SARS-CoV-2 pandemic began in China in late 2019, and by mid-2021 it had caused respiratory illness in more than 151 million people and 3 million deaths globally .

233
00:31:16,614 --> 00:31:22,839
The mortality rate is between 1 and 2 percent in adults, and it increases sharply with age .

234
00:31:22,839 --> 00:31:29,664
Compared with MERS-CoV and SARS-CoV, SARSCoV-2 is more infectious but less deadly.

235
00:31:29,664 --> 00:31:39,464
Variants with increased transmissibility are emerging, and the Delta (B.1.617.2) variant has recently predominated .

236
00:31:39,464 --> 00:31:44,989
Evidence suggests increased disease severity in gravidas who acquire this variant .

237
00:31:44,989 --> 00:31:50,289
SARS-CoV-2 is predominantly transmitted via respiratory droplets.

238
00:31:50,289 --> 00:31:59,614
Asymptomatic or presymptomatic individuals account for 40 to 50 percent of transmissions (He, 2020; Kimball, 2020).

239
00:31:59,614 --> 00:32:06,226
The immune response to natural infection appears to require both humoral and cell-mediated immunity .

240
00:32:06,226 --> 00:32:14,051
Because of the potential for asymptomatic transmission, interventions such as masking and social distancing are recommended .

241
00:32:14,051 --> 00:32:21,101
Maternal Infection COVID-19 symptoms in gravidas do not differ from those in nonpregnant individuals.

242
00:32:21,101 --> 00:32:28,176
An incubation period of approximately 5 to 14 days is followed by fever, cough, and myalgia.

243
00:32:28,176 --> 00:32:34,789
Loss of smell (anosmia) and taste (ageusia) are reported in more than half of patients .

244
00:32:34,789 --> 00:32:39,201
Diarrhea, nausea, and vomiting are somewhat less common.

245
00:32:39,201 --> 00:32:46,139
Lower respiratory involvement develops in some individuals approximately 8 days after symptom onset .

246
00:32:46,139 --> 00:32:55,651
Signs of worsening of disease include dyspnea, tachypnea, decreased oxygen saturation, and rarely, acute respiratory distress syndrome.

247
00:32:55,651 --> 00:33:06,989
Imaging indicative of moderate to severe viral pneumonia reveals multifocal opacities in a predominantly peripheral distribution and ground glass opacities on computed tomography .

248
00:33:06,989 --> 00:33:13,964
Pregnant women may be at higher risk for intensive care unit admission, invasive ventilation, and death .

249
00:33:13,964 --> 00:33:23,989
A study from Parkland Hospital demonstrated that 5 percent of infected gravidas developed severe or critical illness, and diabetes may be a risk factor .

250
00:33:23,989 --> 00:33:33,101
Although COVID-19 was not associated with adverse pregnancy outcomes, women with severe illness experienced a higher rate of preterm birth.

251
00:33:33,101 --> 00:33:43,614
In a surveillance program of COVID-19 in pregnant women, the rate of preterm birth was higher than the general population—13 percent compared with 10 percent .

252
00:33:43,614 --> 00:33:46,301
Figure 67-3.

253
00:33:46,301 --> 00:33:52,726
A. Chest radiograph of a critically ill pregnant woman at 26 weeks’ gestation demonstrating

254
00:33:52,876 --> 00:33:57,863
diffuse bilateral pulmonary opacities with peripheral predominance.

255
00:33:57,813 --> 00:34:09,276
B. Axial image of the chest from a computed tomographic scan demonstrates advanced COVID-19 disease with multiple peripheral lobular consolidations and ground glass opacities.

256
00:34:09,276 --> 00:34:17,288
Fetal Infection Infection of the placenta by SARS-CoV-2 has been demonstrated, but transmission to the fetus is rare .

257
00:34:17,288 --> 00:34:24,663
Microscopic examination of the placenta may demonstrate inflammation and syncytiotrophoblast necrosis .

258
00:34:24,663 --> 00:34:33,751
Standardized criteria have been proposed for determining definite, probable, or possible placental infection by SARSCoV-2 .

259
00:34:33,751 --> 00:34:44,938
When vertical transmission does occur, infected neonates may have asymptomatic, mild, or severe illness (Raschetti, 2020; Sisman, 2020).

260
00:34:44,938 --> 00:34:54,413
Adhikari and associates (2020) demonstrated infection in 3 percent of neonates tested at 24 and 48 hours after birth.

261
00:34:54,413 --> 00:34:58,576
Mechanisms of vertical transmission are poorly understood.

262
00:34:58,576 --> 00:35:03,776
No evidence suggests transmission of SARS-CoV-2 through breastmilk .

263
00:35:03,776 --> 00:35:12,263
Newborns with SARS-CoV-2 do not need to be separated from their mothers but are isolated from the general newborn nursery population.

264
00:35:12,263 --> 00:35:15,038
Figure 67-4.

265
00:35:15,038 --> 00:35:18,338
Placental SARS-CoV-2 infection.

266
00:35:18,338 --> 00:35:24,026
A. Histologic slide of a placenta with known maternal SARS-CoV-2 infection.

267
00:35:24,026 --> 00:35:35,451
Large, discrete areas of the placenta showed increased perivillous fibrin (solid arrow), focal trophoblast necrosis (open arrow), and marked histiocytic inflammation.

268
00:35:35,451 --> 00:35:45,826
B. Anti-SARSCoV-2 viral nucleocapsid antibody is positive in villous trophoblast (brown immunostaining), confirming placental infection.

269
00:35:45,826 --> 00:35:49,738
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Rebecca Collins.

270
00:35:49,738 --> 00:35:57,001
Diagnosis Active maternal infection can be diagnosed from nasopharyngeal, nasal, or saliva specimens.

271
00:35:57,001 --> 00:36:07,976
Reverse transcriptase–polymerase chain reaction (RTPCR) testing identifies viral RNA, whereas rapid antigen testing detects viral antigen.

272
00:36:07,976 --> 00:36:17,338
Several commercial tests are available and listed on the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) (2021a) website.

273
00:36:17,338 --> 00:36:23,401
Rapid antigen tests are inexpensive but less sensitive in asymptomatic individuals.

274
00:36:23,401 --> 00:36:35,976
Thus, test result interpretation depends on pretest probability, epidemiological risk factors, and intended use for diagnostic or screening purposes in health-care or congregate settings.

275
00:36:35,976 --> 00:36:44,101
The latter include dormitories, correctional facilities, nursing homes, and housing shelters providing social services.

276
00:36:44,101 --> 00:36:53,101
Serological screening for IgG is used for epidemiological and seroprevalence studies rather than diagnosis of acute infection.

277
00:36:53,101 --> 00:37:01,113
Management For mild to moderate illness, supportive care is indicated and delivery is reserved for obstetric indications.

278
00:37:01,113 --> 00:37:13,613
For those with mild illness at high risk for clinical progression, anti-SARS-CoV-2 monoclonal antibodies may be administered within 10 days of symptom onset and according to variant predominance .

279
00:37:13,613 --> 00:37:22,176
Pregnancy is considered a high-risk criterion, and use of monoclonal antibodies can be considered in consultation with specialists.

280
00:37:22,176 --> 00:37:28,788
Inpatient observation may be considered for moderate illness, as up to 40 percent may worsen .

281
00:37:28,788 --> 00:37:42,426
For pregnant women with severe to critical illness, inpatient management includes supporting respiratory function, managing comorbid conditions, and, when indicated, coordinating safe delivery with subspecialists .

282
00:37:42,426 --> 00:37:51,601
The goal of respiratory support is to maintain oxygen saturation ≥95 percent and reduce dyspnea and tachypnea .

283
00:37:51,601 --> 00:37:59,101
Prone positioning of gravidas to improve oxygenation in severe cases of COVID-19 pneumonia has been described .

284
00:37:59,101 --> 00:38:06,313
With severe infection, a dysregulated inflammatory response contributes to lung injury and hypoxia.

285
00:38:06,313 --> 00:38:22,226
A United Kingdom trial found decreased mortality rates at 28 days with intravenous or oral dexamethasone, 6 mg daily for up to 10 days in patients requiring supplemental oxygen or mechanical ventilation, compared with usual care .

286
00:38:22,226 --> 00:38:34,851
In the ACTT-1 trial, intravenous remdesivir, a broad-spectrum antiviral agent, was associated with shortened time to recovery among patients requiring lowflow supplemental oxygen .

287
00:38:34,851 --> 00:38:42,163
Remdesivir is approved by the Food and Drug Administration for treatment of COVID-19 in hospitalized adults.

288
00:38:42,163 --> 00:38:43,851
Data on

289
00:38:44,020 --> 00:38:50,832
efficacy and safety of antiviral and biological agents for COVID-19 in pregnancy are limited.

290
00:38:50,782 --> 00:39:01,820
In a study of remdesivir in hospitalized gravidas with COVID-19, no serious adverse events were reported, but elevated serum aminotransaminase levels were common .

291
00:39:01,820 --> 00:39:13,382
Decisions to use currently available therapeutic agents under Emergency Use Authorization are made in consultation with experts and should balance potential benefits and theoretical risks .

292
00:39:13,382 --> 00:39:20,382
Antibiotics for respiratory illness are rarely necessary unless bacterial coinfection is suspected.

293
00:39:20,382 --> 00:39:26,957
Prevention Non-pharmaceutical interventions are mainstays for reducing the spread of SARS-COV-2.

294
00:39:26,957 --> 00:39:33,707
These include universal masking, maintaining physical distance of at least 6 feet, and hand hygiene.

295
00:39:33,707 --> 00:39:42,020
In healthcare settings, airborne and contact isolation precautions are used, particularly when invasive procedures are planned .

296
00:39:42,020 --> 00:39:53,320
Currently available vaccines are highly protective against viral transmission and clinical disease in nonpregnant adults (Baden, 2021; Polack, 2020).

297
00:39:53,320 --> 00:40:02,682
In one study, the BNT162b2 mRNA vaccine was associated with a reduction in COVID-19 diagnosed in pregnancy .

298
00:40:02,682 --> 00:40:15,182
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists and the Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (2021) recommend that pregnant and lactating women receive the COVID-19 vaccine.

299
00:40:15,182 --> 00:40:25,807
Individuals who accept vaccination are counseled to anticipate common reactions such as injection-site pain, headache, fatigue, myalgia, and low-grade fever .

300
00:40:25,807 --> 00:40:32,932
Recent evidence from national experts suggests no greater miscarriage rate in gravidas receiving the vaccine .

301
00:40:32,932 --> 00:40:35,320
# Mumps Virus.

302
00:40:35,320 --> 00:40:45,795
This is an RNA paramyxovirus that primarily infects the salivary glands but also may involve the gonads, meninges, pancreas, and other organs.

303
00:40:45,795 --> 00:40:52,132
It is transmitted by direct contact with respiratory secretions, saliva, or fomites.

304
00:40:52,132 --> 00:41:00,757
Most transmission occurs before and within 5 days of parotitis onset, and droplet isolation is recommended during this time .

305
00:41:00,757 --> 00:41:07,157
Treatment is symptomatic, and mumps during pregnancy is no more severe than in nonpregnant adults.

306
00:41:07,157 --> 00:41:13,970
An increased risk of spontaneous abortion has been observed in women who acquire mumps in the first trimester.

307
00:41:13,970 --> 00:41:20,170
Infection is not associated with congenital malformations, and fetal infection is rare .

308
00:41:20,170 --> 00:41:32,770
Recent outbreaks in previously vaccinated young adults prompted the recommendation of a third dose of measles-mumps-rubella (MMR) vaccine for at-risk nonpregnant individuals during any outbreak .

309
00:41:32,770 --> 00:41:36,382
The MMR vaccine is a live-virus vaccine.

310
00:41:36,382 --> 00:41:43,620
Thus, it is contraindicated in pregnancy, although fetal risk from inadvertent administration is theoretical .

311
00:41:43,620 --> 00:41:47,870
Pregnancy should be avoided for 30 days after vaccination.

312
00:41:47,870 --> 00:41:54,545
The vaccine is encouraged for susceptible postpartum women, and breastfeeding is not contraindicated.

313
00:41:54,545 --> 00:41:57,770
# Measles Virus (Rubeola).

314
00:41:57,770 --> 00:42:04,257
This is a highly contagious RNA virus of the family Paramyxoviridae that infects only humans.

315
00:42:04,257 --> 00:42:13,607
In endemic areas, annual outbreaks of measles occur in late winter and early spring, with larger epidemic cycles every 2 to 5 years.

316
00:42:13,607 --> 00:42:25,482
Although once declared eliminated in the United States, recent outbreaks raise concerns regarding waning population immunity (Patel, 2019; Strebel, 2019).

317
00:42:25,482 --> 00:42:33,507
Transmission is by respiratory droplets, and the secondary attack rate among susceptible contacts exceeds 90 percent .

318
00:42:33,507 --> 00:42:38,932
Typical symptoms include fever, coryza, conjunctivitis, and cough.

319
00:42:38,932 --> 00:42:45,070
Koplik spots, small white lesions with surrounding erythema, appear on the buccal mucosa.

320
00:42:45,070 --> 00:42:53,757
A characteristic erythematous maculopapular rash develops on the face and neck and then spreads to the back, trunk, and extremities.

321
00:42:53,757 --> 00:43:00,670
Diagnosis of acute infection is made by detection of measles-specific IgM antibodies in serum.

322
00:43:00,670 --> 00:43:09,882
Additionally, measles RNA may be detected by RT-PCR testing of a throat swab, nasopharyngeal swab, or urine specimen.

323
00:43:09,882 --> 00:43:16,282
Treatment of measles is supportive, and evaluation for secondary infections may be necessary.

324
00:43:16,282 --> 00:43:30,570
For pregnant women without evidence of measles immunity, postexposure prophylaxis is intravenous immune globulin (IVIG), and a 400mg/kg dose is ideally given within 6 days of exposure .

325
00:43:30,570 --> 00:43:32,845
Human immune globulin

326
00:43:33,004 --> 00:43:43,504
IG) is a blood product used to provide antibodies for short-term prevention of infectious diseases and is prepared from plasma pools derived from thousands of donors.

327
00:43:43,454 --> 00:43:54,829
Measles in pregnancy is associated with increased risk for pregnancy loss, pneumonia, and death (Eberhart-Phillips, 1993; Ogbuanu, 2014).

328
00:43:54,829 --> 00:44:05,179
If a woman develops measles shortly before delivery, risk of serious infection developing in the neonate is considerable, and neonatal chemoprophylaxis is warranted.

329
00:44:05,179 --> 00:44:11,466
As a live-virus vaccine, MMR vaccine is not provided during pregnancy as prevention.

330
00:44:11,466 --> 00:44:17,116
However, MMR vaccination is not an indication for pregnancy termination.

331
00:44:17,116 --> 00:44:23,541
Susceptible women are vaccinated routinely postpartum, and breastfeeding is not contraindicated.

332
00:44:23,541 --> 00:44:26,054
# Rubella Virus.

333
00:44:26,054 --> 00:44:34,341
Also called German measles, rubella is caused by an RNA togavirus and is of minor importance in the absence of pregnancy.

334
00:44:34,341 --> 00:44:42,066
Transmission is via nasopharyngeal secretions, and the transmission rate is 80 percent to nonimmune individuals.

335
00:44:42,066 --> 00:44:46,391
The peak incidence is late winter and spring in endemic areas .

336
00:44:46,391 --> 00:44:53,016
In 2018, more than 26,000 cases of rubella virus were reported worldwide .

337
00:44:53,016 --> 00:45:03,941
In the United States, ongoing or sustained transmission was eliminated in 2004, and large epidemics of rubella have virtually disappeared here because of immunization.

338
00:45:03,941 --> 00:45:12,841
However, up to 10 percent of women are susceptible, and cases are also imported each year from countries with incomplete vaccine coverage .

339
00:45:12,841 --> 00:45:19,891
Maternal rubella is usually a mild febrile illness that follows an incubation of 12 to 23 days.

340
00:45:19,891 --> 00:45:26,179
A generalized maculopapular rash begins on the face and spreads to the trunk and extremities.

341
00:45:26,179 --> 00:45:33,754
Other symptoms may include arthralgias or arthritis, head and neck lymphadenopathy, and conjunctivitis.

342
00:45:33,754 --> 00:45:38,916
However, 25 to 50 percent of infections are asymptomatic.

343
00:45:38,916 --> 00:45:48,616
Viremia usually precedes clinical signs by approximately a week, and adults are infectious during viremia and through 7 days after the rash appears.

344
00:45:48,616 --> 00:45:58,616
Up to half of maternal infections are subclinical despite viremia that may cause spontaneous abortion or devastating fetal infection, described subsequently.

345
00:45:58,616 --> 00:46:08,629
Diagnosis Rubella virus may be isolated from the urine, blood, nasopharynx, and cerebrospinal fluid for up to 2 weeks after rash onset.

346
00:46:08,629 --> 00:46:13,754
However, the diagnosis is usually made with serological analysis.

347
00:46:13,754 --> 00:46:26,866
Specific IgM antibody can be detected using enzyme-linked immunoassay for 4 to 5 days after onset of clinical disease, but antibody can persist for up to 6 weeks after appearance of the rash.

348
00:46:26,866 --> 00:46:33,304
Importantly, rubella virus reinfection can give rise to transient low levels of IgM.

349
00:46:33,304 --> 00:46:37,316
With this, no adverse fetal effects have been described.

350
00:46:37,316 --> 00:46:43,654
A false-positive IgM result is also possible in populations with low prevalence of disease.

351
00:46:43,654 --> 00:46:49,241
Serum IgG antibody titers peak 1 to 2 weeks after rash onset.

352
00:46:49,241 --> 00:46:57,704
These may fail to distinguish between recent infection and preexisting immunity if the specimen is obtained more than a week after symptoms.

353
00:46:57,704 --> 00:47:06,529
IgG avidity testing aids differentiation, and high-avidity IgG antibodies indicate an infection at least 2 months prior.

354
00:47:06,529 --> 00:47:14,191
Fetal Effects Rubella virus is one of the most complete teratogens, and its effects are worst during organogenesis.

355
00:47:14,191 --> 00:47:21,741
Up to 90 percent of pregnant women with rubella and a rash during the first 12 weeks of gestation have an affected fetus .

356
00:47:21,741 --> 00:47:30,604
At 13 to 14 weeks’ gestation, this incidence is 50 percent, and by the end of the second trimester, it is 25 percent.

357
00:47:30,604 --> 00:47:34,191
Defects are rare after 20 weeks’ gestation.

358
00:47:34,191 --> 00:47:45,041
Features of congenital rubella syndrome are cardiac septal defects, pulmonary stenosis, microcephaly, cataracts, microphthalmia, and hepatosplenomegaly .

359
00:47:45,041 --> 00:47:53,816
Other abnormalities include sensorineural deafness, intellectual disability, neonatal purpura, and radiolucent bone disease.

360
00:47:53,816 --> 00:48:03,016
Neonates born with congenital rubella may shed the virus for many months and thus be a threat to other infants and to susceptible adults who contact them.

361
00:48:03,016 --> 00:48:07,316
Management and Prevention Rubella has no specific treatment.

362
00:48:07,316 --> 00:48:12,554
Droplet precautions for 7 days after the onset of the rash are recommended.

363
00:48:12,554 --> 00:48:18,816
Postexposure passive immunization with IVIG may be of benefit if given within 5

364
00:48:18,988 --> 00:48:20,988
days of exposure .

365
00:48:20,938 --> 00:48:31,563
When rubella infection is diagnosed in the mother, targeted sonographic examination is performed to evaluate for fetal structural anomalies described in the last section.

366
00:48:31,563 --> 00:48:36,825
Referral to and counseling by a maternal–fetal medicine specialist is warranted.

367
00:48:36,825 --> 00:48:49,425
If ultrasound findings suggest congenital infection or growth delay, diagnostic amniocentesis is recommended to detect rubella virus by PCR and to test for other congenital infections.

368
00:48:49,425 --> 00:48:57,275
As prevention, MMR vaccine should be offered to nonpregnant women of childbearing age who lack evidence of immunity.

369
00:48:57,275 --> 00:49:06,325
Vaccination of all susceptible hospital personnel who might be exposed to patients with rubella or who might have contact with pregnant women is important.

370
00:49:06,325 --> 00:49:17,025
Rubella vaccination is avoided 1 month before or during pregnancy because the vaccine contains attenuated live virus, although the fetal malformation risk is theoretical.

371
00:49:17,025 --> 00:49:21,850
MMR vaccination is not an indication for pregnancy termination.

372
00:49:21,850 --> 00:49:27,338
Prenatal serological screening for rubella is indicated for all pregnant women.

373
00:49:27,338 --> 00:49:32,263
Women found to be nonimmune are offered the MMR vaccine postpartum.

374
00:49:32,263 --> 00:49:35,250
# Parvovirus B19.

375
00:49:35,250 --> 00:49:39,838
This virus causes erythema infectiosum or fifth disease.

376
00:49:39,838 --> 00:49:47,988
It is a small, singlestranded DNA virus that replicates in rapidly proliferating cells such as erythroblast precursors.

377
00:49:47,988 --> 00:49:52,425
This can lead to anemia, which is its primary fetal effect.

378
00:49:52,425 --> 00:50:01,500
In individuals with increased red cell turnover, such as with sicklecell disease, parvovirus infection may cause an aplastic crisis.

379
00:50:01,500 --> 00:50:11,213
The primary mode of parvovirus transmission is respiratory secretions, although infection through blood transfusion or organ donation has been reported.

380
00:50:11,213 --> 00:50:15,163
Most infections occur in late winter to early spring.

381
00:50:15,163 --> 00:50:21,025
The maternal infection rate is highest in women with school-aged children and in day-care workers.

382
00:50:21,025 --> 00:50:27,350
Viremia develops 4 to 14 days after exposure, and a rash follows several days later.

383
00:50:27,350 --> 00:50:33,400
An otherwise immunocompetent individual is no longer infectious at the onset of the rash.

384
00:50:33,400 --> 00:50:37,550
By adulthood, only 40 percent of women are susceptible.

385
00:50:37,550 --> 00:50:44,525
The annual seroconversion rate is 1 to 2 percent, and the secondary attack rate approaches 50 percent.

386
00:50:44,525 --> 00:50:50,475
Maternal Infection In 20 to 30 percent of adults, infection is asymptomatic.

387
00:50:50,475 --> 00:50:57,438
In others, fever, headache, and flu-like symptoms may begin in the last few days of the viremic phase.

388
00:50:57,438 --> 00:51:04,488
Several days later, a bright red rash with erythroderma affects the face and gives a slapped-cheek appearance.

389
00:51:04,488 --> 00:51:08,988
The rash becomes lacelike and spreads to the trunk and extremities.

390
00:51:08,988 --> 00:51:16,025
Adults often have milder rashes and develop symmetrical polyarthralgia that may persist for several weeks.

391
00:51:16,025 --> 00:51:21,250
No evidence suggests that parvovirus infection is altered by pregnancy.

392
00:51:21,250 --> 00:51:30,025
With recovery, IgM antibody is generated 7 to 10 days postinfection, and production persists for 3 to 4 months.

393
00:51:30,025 --> 00:51:38,150
Several days after IgM is produced, IgG antibody is detectable and persists for life with natural immunity .

394
00:51:38,150 --> 00:51:49,100
Fetal Infection There is vertical transmission to the fetus in up to a third of maternal parvovirus infections (de Jong, 2011; Lamont, 2011).

395
00:51:49,100 --> 00:51:54,900
Fetal infection is associated with abortion, nonimmune hydrops, and stillbirth .

396
00:51:54,900 --> 00:52:05,675
The fetal loss rate with serologically proven parvovirus infection is 8 to 17 percent before 20 weeks’ gestation, and 2 to 6 percent after midpregnancy .

397
00:52:05,675 --> 00:52:13,725
Currently, no data support screening asymptomatic mothers or evaluating stillborn fetuses for parvovirus infection.

398
00:52:13,725 --> 00:52:16,575
Figure 67-5.

399
00:52:16,575 --> 00:52:24,975
Stillborn, hydropic fetus and characteristically large placenta resulting from intrauterine parvovirus B19 infection.

400
00:52:24,975 --> 00:52:28,775
Reproduced with permission from Dr. April Bleich.

401
00:52:28,775 --> 00:52:35,125
Fetal hydrops develops in approximately 4 percent of gravidas infected with parvovirus .

402
00:52:35,125 --> 00:52:41,538
Still, it is the most frequent infectious cause of nonimmune hydrops in autopsied fetuses .

403
00:52:41,538 --> 00:52:46,225
Hydrops usually stems from infection in the first half of gestation.

404
00:52:46,225 --> 00:52:53,663
In one report, more than 80 percent of cases of fetal infection developed within 10 weeks of maternal infection.

405
00:52:53,663 --> 00:52:56,813
The mean interval was 6 to 7 weeks .

406
00:52:56,813 --> 00:53:04,563
The critical period for maternal infection leading to fetal hydrops is estimated to be between 13 and 16 weeks’

407
00:53:04,732 --> 00:53:10,969
gestation, which coincides with the period in which fetal hepatic hemopoiesis is greatest .

408
00:53:10,919 --> 00:53:20,107
Diagnosis and Management An algorithm for diagnosis of maternal parvoviral infection is illustrated in Figure 67-6.

409
00:53:20,107 --> 00:53:27,907
Diagnosis is generally made by maternal serological testing for specific IgG and IgM antibodies.

410
00:53:27,907 --> 00:53:36,444
Viral DNA may be detectable by PCR testing in maternal serum during the prodrome and persist for months to years after infection.

411
00:53:36,444 --> 00:53:42,819
Fetal infection is diagnosed by detection of B19 viral DNA in amnionic fluid .

412
00:53:42,819 --> 00:53:50,082
Fetal and maternal viral loads do not predict fetal morbidity and mortality (de Haan, 2007).

413
00:53:50,082 --> 00:53:52,932
Figure 67-6.

414
00:53:52,932 --> 00:53:59,482
Algorithm for evaluation and management of human parvovirus B19 infection in pregnancy.

415
00:53:59,482 --> 00:54:16,019
CBC = complete blood count; IgG = immunoglobulin G; IgM = immunoglobulin M; MCA = middle cerebral artery; PCR = polymerase chain reaction; RNA = ribonucleic acid.

416
00:54:16,019 --> 00:54:22,407
Most cases of parvovirus-associated hydrops develop in the first 10 weeks after infection.

417
00:54:22,407 --> 00:54:28,007
Thus, sonography should be performed every 2 weeks in women with recent infection.

418
00:54:28,007 --> 00:54:38,157
As discussed in Chapter 14 (p. 262), middle cerebral artery (MCA) Doppler interrogation is used to predict fetal anemia.

419
00:54:38,157 --> 00:54:44,932
MCA Doppler findings that suggest anemia may warrant fetal blood sampling followed by transfusion.

420
00:54:44,932 --> 00:54:51,444
In hydropic fetuses without transfusion, mortality rates up to 30 percent have been reported.

421
00:54:51,444 --> 00:55:01,019
With transfusion, 94 percent of hydrops cases resolve within 6 to 12 weeks, and the overall mortality rate is <10 percent.

422
00:55:01,019 --> 00:55:07,944
Most fetuses require only one transfusion because hemopoiesis resumes as infection resolves.

423
00:55:07,944 --> 00:55:16,182
Long-term Prognosis Neurodevelopmental outcomes in fetuses transfused for B19 infectioninduced anemia have varied.

424
00:55:16,182 --> 00:55:27,757
In one review of 24 transfused hydropic fetuses, abnormal neurodevelopment was noted in 5 of 16 survivors—32 percent—at 6 months to 8 years .

425
00:55:27,757 --> 00:55:38,082
Outcomes were not related to severity of fetal anemia or acidemia, and these investigators hypothesized that the infection itself induced cerebral damage.

426
00:55:38,082 --> 00:55:51,194
In another study of 28 children treated with intrauterine transfusion, 11 percent had neurodevelopmental impairment during evaluation at a median age of 5 years (de Jong, 2012).

427
00:55:51,194 --> 00:55:59,594
Conversely, Dembinski (2003) found no significant neurodevelopmental delay despite severe fetal anemia.

428
00:55:59,594 --> 00:56:09,857
Prevention Currently, no parvovirus vaccine is available, and no antiviral agent is recommended for prevention or treatment of maternal or fetal infection.

429
00:56:09,857 --> 00:56:16,407
Decisions to avoid higher-risk work settings are complex and require assessment of exposure risks.

430
00:56:16,407 --> 00:56:32,407
Pregnant women should be counseled that risks for infection approximate 5 percent for casual, infrequent contact; 20 percent for intense, prolonged work exposure such as for teachers; and 50 percent for close, frequent interaction such as in the home.

431
00:56:32,407 --> 00:56:40,307
Workers at day-care centers and schools need not avoid infected children because infectivity is greatest before clinical illness.

432
00:56:40,307 --> 00:56:44,557
Last, infected children do not require isolation.

433
00:56:44,557 --> 00:56:47,782
# Other Respiratory Viruses.

434
00:56:47,782 --> 00:56:57,382
More than 200 antigenically distinct respiratory viruses cause the common cold, pharyngitis, laryngitis, bronchitis, and pneumonia.

435
00:56:57,382 --> 00:57:05,557
The DNA-containing adenovirus is more likely to produce cough and lower respiratory tract involvement, including pneumonia.

436
00:57:05,557 --> 00:57:18,244
The RNA-containing rhinovirus, respiratory enterovirus, and nonpandemic common coronavirus usually produce a trivial illness characterized by rhinorrhea, sneezing, and congestion.

437
00:57:18,244 --> 00:57:30,794
An enterovirus named EV-D68 caused large outbreaks of respiratory illness in several countries and was linked to clusters of poliolike illness in children, known as acute flaccid myelitis .

438
00:57:30,794 --> 00:57:35,007
The teratogenic risks of respiratory viruses are low.

439
00:57:35,007 --> 00:57:45,157
In one study, amnionic fluid viral PCR testing was done on samples from 1191 women undergoing amniocentesis for fetal karyotyping .

440
00:57:45,157 --> 00:57:53,082
Viral PCR was positive in 6.5 percent, and adenovirus was the virus most frequently identified.

441
00:57:53,082 --> 00:58:01,782
There was an association with fetal-growth restriction, nonimmune hydrops, foot/hand abnormalities, and neural-tube defects.

442
00:58:01,782 --> 00:58:06,869
Adenoviral infection is a known cause of childhood myocarditis and

443
00:58:07,036 --> 00:58:12,298
has been reported in cases of fetal myocarditis and nonimmune hydrops .

444
00:58:12,248 --> 00:58:15,073
# Other Enteroviruses.

445
00:58:15,073 --> 00:58:31,686
Characterized by a clinical phenotype distinct from that of respiratory enteroviruses, these viruses are a major subgroup of RNA picornaviruses that include nonrespiratory enterovirus species, coxsackievirus, poliovirus, and echovirus.

446
00:58:31,686 --> 00:58:43,061
They are trophic for intestinal epithelium but can also cause widespread maternal, fetal, and neonatal infections that may include the CNS, skin, heart, and lungs.

447
00:58:43,061 --> 00:58:48,423
Most maternal infections are subclinical yet can be fatal to the fetus-neonate .

448
00:58:48,423 --> 00:58:54,786
Hepatitis A is an enterovirus discussed in Chapter 58 (p. 1036).

449
00:58:54,786 --> 00:58:59,961
Coxsackievirus infections with group A and B are usually asymptomatic.

450
00:58:59,961 --> 00:59:14,461
Symptomatic infections—usually with group B—include aseptic meningitis; polio-like illness; hand, foot, and mouth disease; rashes; respiratory disease; pleuritis; pericarditis; and myocarditis.

451
00:59:14,461 --> 00:59:17,623
No treatment or vaccination is available .

452
00:59:17,623 --> 00:59:26,411
Coxsackievirus may be transmitted by maternal secretions to the fetus at delivery in up to half of mothers who seroconverted during pregnancy .

453
00:59:26,411 --> 00:59:35,611
Transplacental passage also has been reported, which may cause neonatal skin lesions, myocarditis, pneumonia, or encephalitis .

454
00:59:35,611 --> 00:59:43,173
Some have reported higher rates of cardiac anomalies and of low-birthweight, preterm, and growth-restricted newborns .

455
00:59:43,173 --> 00:59:48,723
Polioviruses are highly contagious and cause subclinical or mild infections.

456
00:59:48,723 --> 00:59:54,298
The virus is trophic for the brain, and it can cause paralytic poliomyelitis .

457
00:59:54,298 --> 01:00:03,023
Siegel (1955) demonstrated that pregnant women not only were more susceptible to polio but also had a higher death rate.

458
01:00:03,023 --> 01:00:09,848
Perinatal transmission has been observed, especially when maternal infection developed in the third trimester .

459
01:00:09,848 --> 01:00:20,248
Inactivated subcutaneous polio vaccine is recommended for susceptible pregnant women who must travel to endemic areas or are placed in other high-risk situations.

460
01:00:20,248 --> 01:00:27,298
Live oral polio vaccine has been used for mass vaccination during pregnancy without harmful fetal effects .

461
01:00:27,298 --> 01:00:30,261
# Mosquito-borne Viruses.

462
01:00:30,261 --> 01:00:35,961
West Nile Virus This mosquito-borne RNA flavivirus is a human neuropathogen.

463
01:00:35,961 --> 01:00:40,936
It is the most common cause of arthropod-borne disease in the United States .

464
01:00:40,936 --> 01:00:48,861
West Nile viral infection is typically acquired through mosquito bites and is rarely transmitted through donated blood or organs.

465
01:00:48,861 --> 01:00:55,298
The incubation period is 2 to 14 days, and most persons have mild or no symptoms.

466
01:00:55,298 --> 01:01:02,136
Less than 1 percent of infected adults develop meningoencephalitis or acute flaccid paralysis.

467
01:01:02,136 --> 01:01:09,211
With these, presenting symptoms may include fever, mental status changes, muscle weakness, and coma .

468
01:01:09,211 --> 01:01:19,323
West Nile infection is diagnosed based on clinical symptoms and detection of viral IgG and IgM in serum and IgM in cerebrospinal fluid .

469
01:01:19,323 --> 01:01:24,498
Management is supportive, and no effective antiviral treatment is available.

470
01:01:24,498 --> 01:01:35,036
The primary strategy for preventing exposure in pregnancy is use of insect repellant containing N,N-diethyl-m-toluamide (DEET), which is considered safe .

471
01:01:35,036 --> 01:01:41,211
Avoiding outdoor activity and stagnant water and wearing protective clothing also are recommended.

472
01:01:41,211 --> 01:01:45,911
Adverse effects of West Nile viremia on pregnancy are unclear.

473
01:01:45,911 --> 01:02:01,923
Animal data suggest that embryos are susceptible, and one case report of human fetal infection at 27 weeks’ gestation described chorioretinitis and severe cerebral leukomalacia (Alpert, 2003; Julander, 2006).

474
01:02:01,923 --> 01:02:15,348
However, data from the West Nile Virus Pregnancy Registry show no increased risk for miscarriage, preterm birth, or major malformations (O’Leary, 2006; Pridjian, 2016).

475
01:02:15,348 --> 01:02:21,986
No evidence suggests that maternal West Nile virus infection is harmful to a breastfeeding infant.

476
01:02:21,986 --> 01:02:28,323
Zika Virus This RNA flavivirus is the first major mosquito-borne teratogen recognized .

477
01:02:28,323 --> 01:02:34,811
Zika virus is primarily transmitted by mosquito bite, but sexual transmission is possible.

478
01:02:34,811 --> 01:02:44,511
The virus may be detected in body fluids for months following acute infection (Joguet, 2017; Paz-Bailey, 2017).

479
01:02:44,511 --> 01:02:54,636
It was first introduced to a nonimmune Brazilian population in 2014, but as of 2020, no countries had reported active Zika transmission.

480
01:02:54,636 --> 01:02:57,061
Maternal-Fetal Infection.

481
01:02:57,061 --> 01:03:00,298
Zika infection is asymptomatic in

482
01:03:00,460 --> 01:03:03,610
approximately 80 percent of adults.

483
01:03:03,560 --> 01:03:10,610
It may cause mild rash, fever, headache, arthralgia, and conjunctivitis lasting a few days.

484
01:03:10,610 --> 01:03:15,410
The fetus can be severely infected whether or not the mother is symptomatic.

485
01:03:15,410 --> 01:03:23,622
In one report of 134 women with positive RT-PCR results, the fetal mortality rate was 7 percent .

486
01:03:23,622 --> 01:03:31,285
Among live births with first-trimester exposure, the rate of fetal birth defects ranges from 5 to 15 percent .

487
01:03:31,285 --> 01:03:46,447
In the most severely affected fetuses, a congenital Zika syndrome has been described that includes microcephaly, lissencephaly, ventriculomegaly, intracranial calcifications, ocular abnormalities, and congenital contractures .

488
01:03:46,447 --> 01:03:48,910
Diagnosis and Management.

489
01:03:48,910 --> 01:03:56,510
Zika virus is typically detectable in blood around the time of symptom onset and may persist days to months in pregnant women .

490
01:03:56,510 --> 01:04:03,422
Serum IgM antibodies typically become detectable within the first 2 weeks and may persist for months.

491
01:04:03,422 --> 01:04:09,622
IgG antibodies develop shortly after IgM ones and confer long-lived immunity .

492
01:04:09,622 --> 01:04:21,572
Laboratory testing for Zika infection is recommended only in symptomatic gravidas with residence in or recent travel to areas with active dengue transmission and risk for Zika transmission .

493
01:04:21,572 --> 01:04:31,810
For a pregnant woman within 12 weeks of symptom onset, PCR testing of serum and urine specimens for both dengue and Zika virus RNA is recommended.

494
01:04:31,810 --> 01:04:42,347
For women with prenatal ultrasound findings suggesting congenital Zika syndrome, Zika virus PCR and IgM are initially performed on serum and urine.

495
01:04:42,347 --> 01:04:50,472
Amnionic fluid PCR may be performed, although less is known about its diagnostic accuracy for congenital infection.

496
01:04:50,472 --> 01:04:58,460
Currently, no specific treatment or vaccine is available for Zika infection, although several vaccines are in development .

497
01:04:58,460 --> 01:05:09,710
Prophylaxis includes protective netting, insect spray to control the vector mosquito, personal DEET spray use, and avoidance of sexual contact with partners recently exposed.

498
01:05:09,710 --> 01:05:12,122
# Ebola Virus.

499
01:05:12,122 --> 01:05:19,585
A member of the RNA Filoviridae family, the Ebola virus is transmitted by direct person-to-person contact .

500
01:05:19,585 --> 01:05:27,735
Infection produces a severe hemorrhagic fever with pronounced immunosuppression and disseminated intravascular coagulopathy.

501
01:05:27,735 --> 01:05:29,897
Treatment is supportive.

502
01:05:29,897 --> 01:05:38,235
Data are few concerning Ebola viral infection in gravidas (Money, 2015; Oduyebo, 2015).

503
01:05:38,235 --> 01:05:51,485
Henwood and associates (2017) described an approximate 50-percent risk of death among pregnant and nonpregnant women with Ebola virus disease, and universally poor fetal and neonatal outcomes.

504
01:05:51,485 --> 01:05:58,172
That said, no evidence suggests that pregnant women are more susceptible to Ebola virus infection.

505
01:05:58,172 --> 01:06:06,572
In survivors, persistent Ebola virus has been detected months after recovery in “sanctuary sites” such as the eyes and gonads.

506
01:06:06,572 --> 01:06:17,410
A live-attenuated Ebola virus vaccine, rVSV-ZEBOV, was approved in 2019 in the United States for individuals studying or treating Ebola virus.

507
01:06:17,410 --> 01:06:30,660
Among 84 health-care workers who inadvertently received vaccination in early pregnancy or who became pregnant soon after vaccination, pregnancy loss or congenital anomalies were not associated with the vaccine .

508
01:06:30,660 --> 01:06:33,022
Bacterial Infections.

509
01:06:33,022 --> 01:06:35,897
# Group A Streptococcus.

510
01:06:35,897 --> 01:06:41,660
Streptococcus pyogenes is the most frequent bacterial cause of acute pharyngitis.

511
01:06:41,660 --> 01:06:48,622
In most cases, streptococcal pharyngitis, scarlet fever, and erysipelas are not life threatening.

512
01:06:48,622 --> 01:06:54,310
Treatment, usually with penicillin, is equivalent for pregnant and nonpregnant women.

513
01:06:54,310 --> 01:07:01,522
S pyogenes produces numerous toxins and enzymes responsible for its local and systemic toxicity.

514
01:07:01,522 --> 01:07:07,285
Pyrogenic exotoxin-producing strains are usually associated with severe disease .

515
01:07:07,285 --> 01:07:12,785
In the United States, S pyogenes infrequently causes puerperal infection.

516
01:07:12,785 --> 01:07:25,285
Still, it remains the most common cause of severe maternal postpartum infection and death worldwide, and their incidence is rising (Hamilton, 2013; Wessels, 2018).

517
01:07:25,285 --> 01:07:31,522
Puerperal infections are discussed in detail in Chapter 37 (p. 649).

518
01:07:31,522 --> 01:07:34,460
# Group B Streptococcus.

519
01:07:34,460 --> 01:07:42,747
Streptococcus agalactiae colonizes the gastrointestinal and genitourinary tract in 10 to 25 percent of gravidas .

520
01:07:42,747 --> 01:07:50,522
Group B Streptococcus (GBS) is isolated in a transient, intermittent, or chronic fashion during pregnancy.

521
01:07:50,522 --> 01:07:53,047
Although the organism is more

522
01:07:53,212 --> 01:07:58,924
likely always present in these same women, its isolation is not always consistent.

523
01:07:58,874 --> 01:08:06,674
Maternal and Perinatal Infection Maternal and fetal GBS effects range from asymptomatic colonization to septicemia.

524
01:08:06,674 --> 01:08:16,437
S agalactiae can cause maternal bacteriuria, pyelonephritis, osteomyelitis, postpartum mastitis, and puerperal infections.

525
01:08:16,437 --> 01:08:25,574
GBS may also have the ability to overcome normally protective cervical mucus barriers during pregnancy to cause ascending antenatal infection .

526
01:08:25,574 --> 01:08:40,062
As such, GBS has also been implicated in preterm labor, prelabor rupture of membranes, chorioamnionitis, fetal infections, and stillbirth (Randis, 2014; Seale, 2017).

527
01:08:40,062 --> 01:08:47,399
Among newborns in the United States, GBS remains the leading infectious cause of morbidity and mortality .

528
01:08:47,399 --> 01:09:00,949
Infection <7 days after birth is defined as early-onset disease, although many investigators recognize a threshold of <72 hours of life as most compatible with intrapartum acquisition of disease .

529
01:09:00,949 --> 01:09:12,412
Tudela and associates (2012) reported that newborns with early-onset GBS infection often had clinical evidence of fetal infection during labor or at delivery.

530
01:09:12,412 --> 01:09:20,337
In many neonates, septicemia involves signs of serious illness that usually develop within 6 to 12 hours of birth.

531
01:09:20,337 --> 01:09:25,137
These include respiratory distress, apnea, and hypotension.

532
01:09:25,137 --> 01:09:34,487
At the outset, therefore, neonatal infection must be differentiated from respiratory distress syndrome caused by insufficient surfactant production .

533
01:09:34,487 --> 01:09:43,062
The mortality rate with early-onset disease has declined to approximately 4 percent, and preterm newborns are disparately affected.

534
01:09:43,062 --> 01:09:54,012
Late-onset disease caused by GBS is noted in 0.28 per 1000 live births and usually manifests as meningitis 1 week to 3 months after birth .

535
01:09:54,012 --> 01:10:01,174
The mortality rate, although appreciable, is lower for late-onset meningitis than for early-onset sepsis.

536
01:10:01,174 --> 01:10:10,262
Unfortunately, it is not uncommon for surviving infants of both early- and late-onset disease to exhibit devastating neurological sequelae.

537
01:10:10,262 --> 01:10:19,262
Prophylaxis for Perinatal Infections Maternal intrapartum antibiotic administration is the main prevention of early-onset GBS disease.

538
01:10:19,262 --> 01:10:32,924
In the United States, rates of early-onset neonatal sepsis decreased from 3 cases per 1000 live births in the early 1990s to 0.25 cases in the years following widespread prophylaxis .

539
01:10:32,924 --> 01:10:44,712
Both culture- and risk-based criteria to identify candidates for intrapartum prophylaxis have been proposed and debated (Braye, 2019; Hughes, 2017).

540
01:10:44,712 --> 01:10:47,362
Table 67-2.

541
01:10:47,362 --> 01:10:53,862
Indications for Intrapartum Group B Streptococcal Infection Prophylaxisa Culture-based Prevention.

542
01:10:53,862 --> 01:11:05,849
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020b) recommends universal vaginal-rectal culture screening for GBS at 36 to 38 weeks’ gestation.

543
01:11:05,849 --> 01:11:12,174
This is followed by intrapartum antibiotic prophylaxis for women identified to be carriers.

544
01:11:12,174 --> 01:11:19,562
Selective enrichment of the vaginal-rectal culture to favor GBS growth and then subculture improves detection.

545
01:11:19,562 --> 01:11:27,162
GBS isolates can also be tested for inducible clindamycin resistance in women with reported penicillin allergy.

546
01:11:27,162 --> 01:11:39,637
Updated guidance also recommends consideration of antenatal maternal penicillin-allergy testing in GBS-colonized women at high risk for anaphylaxis or IgE-mediated hypersensitivity .

547
01:11:39,637 --> 01:11:51,012
In a recent surveillance study of neonates with early-onset sepsis, 53 percent of those diagnosed with GBS disease were born to mothers with negative antenatal screening results .

548
01:11:51,012 --> 01:12:02,274
In women with heavy colonization, GBS may be specifically identified in a routine prenatal urine culture obtained to exclude asymptomatic bacteriuria (ASB).

549
01:12:02,274 --> 01:12:13,512
Cultures with ≥105 colony-forming units (cfu) should prompt treatment of ASB to reduce the risk of acute pyelonephritis in pregnancy.

550
01:12:13,512 --> 01:12:20,062
Values <105 do not require acute antepartum antibiotics for ASB.

551
01:12:20,062 --> 01:12:24,424
However, women are given GBS prophylaxis in labor .

552
01:12:24,424 --> 01:12:35,662
As a surrogate, we also consider a urine culture result that identifies gram-positive cocci, without further speciation, to be a presumptive positive result for GBS.

553
01:12:35,662 --> 01:12:37,862
Risk-based Prevention.

554
01:12:37,862 --> 01:12:43,787
This approach is recommended for women in labor and whose GBS culture results are not known.

555
01:12:43,787 --> 01:12:46,987
It relies on risk factors associated

556
01:12:47,140 --> 01:12:50,315
with intrapartum GBS transmission.

557
01:12:50,265 --> 01:12:57,327
Intrapartum chemoprophylaxis is then given to candidates with criteria listed in Table 67-2.

558
01:12:57,327 --> 01:13:02,765
At Parkland Hospital we use the risk-based approach for intrapartum prophylaxis.

559
01:13:02,765 --> 01:13:14,515
Additionally, all term neonates who were not given intrapartum prophylaxis are treated in the delivery room with aqueous penicillin G, 50,000 to 60,000 units intramuscularly.

560
01:13:14,515 --> 01:13:28,840
With this strategy, early-onset neonatal sepsis rates in our predominantly minority population decreased to levels similar to those reported by national surveillance data (Stafford, 2012; Wendel, 2002).

561
01:13:28,840 --> 01:13:31,627
Nucleic Acid–based Testing.

562
01:13:31,627 --> 01:13:38,390
Approximately 20 percent of laboratories now offer PCR testing for maternal GBS screening .

563
01:13:38,390 --> 01:13:46,577
Additionally, a rapid point-of-care PCR test has been evaluated for intrapartum identification of maternal GBS .

564
01:13:46,577 --> 01:13:53,390
One major limitation is cost, which is currently several times greater than that of culture-based techniques.

565
01:13:53,390 --> 01:14:00,040
A second is the inability to determine antibiotic sensitivities using current PCR techniques.

566
01:14:00,040 --> 01:14:09,177
GBS Vaccine Serotype-specific capsular antibody concentrations clinically correlate with reduced rates of GBS neonatal disease.

567
01:14:09,177 --> 01:14:14,902
However, vaccine development is challenged by the diversity of GBS serotypes.

568
01:14:14,902 --> 01:14:22,052
Another hurdle is the bacterium’s ability to undergo serotype switching by altering its capsular polysaccharide .

569
01:14:22,052 --> 01:14:25,652
No vaccines are clinically available at this time.

570
01:14:25,652 --> 01:14:35,127
Intrapartum Antimicrobial Prophylaxis Penicillin remains the first-line agent for prophylaxis, and ampicillin is an acceptable alternative .

571
01:14:35,127 --> 01:14:41,140
Women with a penicillin allergy and at low risk for anaphylaxis are given cefazolin .

572
01:14:41,140 --> 01:14:55,327
Those at high risk for anaphylaxis or other life-threatening reaction, such as Stevens-Johnson syndrome, should have antimicrobial susceptibility testing for erythromycin and clindamycin performed on GBS isolates.

573
01:14:55,327 --> 01:15:02,890
Clindamycin-sensitive but erythromycin-resistant isolates should undergo inducible clindamycin resistance testing.

574
01:15:02,890 --> 01:15:08,202
If clindamycin resistance is confirmed, vancomycin should be administered.

575
01:15:08,202 --> 01:15:12,952
Erythromycin is no longer used for penicillin-allergic patients.

576
01:15:12,952 --> 01:15:15,602
Table 67-3.

577
01:15:15,602 --> 01:15:30,640
Regimens for Intrapartum Antimicrobial Prophylaxis for Perinatal GBS Disease Management of spontaneous preterm labor, threatened preterm delivery, or preterm prelabor rupture of membranes includes obtaining a GBS culture.

578
01:15:30,640 --> 01:15:35,590
Intrapartum chemoprophylaxis is initiated until the culture results.

579
01:15:35,590 --> 01:15:47,177
Women undergoing cesarean delivery before labor onset with intact membranes do not need GBS chemoprophylaxis, regardless of colonization status or gestational age.

580
01:15:47,177 --> 01:15:52,165
# Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus Aureus (MRSA).

581
01:15:52,165 --> 01:15:59,990
Staphylococcus aureus is a pyogenic gram-positive organism and is considered the most virulent staphylococcal species.

582
01:15:59,990 --> 01:16:06,040
It primarily colonizes the nares, skin, genital tissues, and oropharynx.

583
01:16:06,040 --> 01:16:13,365
Although approximately 30 percent of adults carry S aureus, MRSA colonizes 2 percent of adults .

584
01:16:13,365 --> 01:16:19,140
MRSA colonization is considered the greatest risk factor for subsequent infection .

585
01:16:19,140 --> 01:16:25,215
MRSA infection may be hospital- or community-acquired MRSA (CAMRSA).

586
01:16:25,215 --> 01:16:34,702
CA-MRSA is diagnosed in an outpatient setting or within 48 hours of hospitalization in a person without health-care exposures in the past year.

587
01:16:34,702 --> 01:16:42,252
Exposures include prior hospitalization, dialysis or surgery, or indwelling catheters or devices .

588
01:16:42,252 --> 01:16:50,302
In pregnant women, most cases are CAMRSA, which disproportionately affects persons in lower socioeconomical strata .

589
01:16:50,302 --> 01:16:58,202
MRSA and Pregnancy Skin and soft tissue infections are the most common presentation of MRSA in pregnant women.

590
01:16:58,202 --> 01:17:08,402
Mastitis and breast abscesses have been reported in up to a fourth of MRSA cases in pregnancy (Laibl, 2005; Lee, 2010).

591
01:17:08,402 --> 01:17:22,990
Perineal or episiotomy abscess, abdominal surgical site infection, and rare placental abscess and chorioamnionitis have been associated with MRSA (Maeda, 2018; Pimentel, 2009).

592
01:17:22,990 --> 01:17:33,427
Last, osteomyelitis and spinal-epidural abscess have been topics of case reports (Tanamai, 2016; Vakili, 2017).

593
01:17:33,427 --> 01:17:36,927
Vertical transmission of MRSA is rare.

594
01:17:36,927 --> 01:17:43,990
Postnatal colonization and infection in the newborn are associated with maternal and health-care worker colonization

595
01:17:44,140 --> 01:17:51,127
or infection near the time of delivery (Jimenez-Truque, 2012; Reich, 2016).

596
01:17:51,077 --> 01:17:58,190
Management Uncomplicated superficial infections are primarily managed by drainage and local wound care.

597
01:17:58,190 --> 01:18:11,140
Although historically de-emphasized, evidence suggests benefit from antibiotic therapy added to incision and drainage of smaller abscesses (Daum, 2017; Forcade, 2012).

598
01:18:11,140 --> 01:18:22,702
Severe superficial infections, especially those that fail to respond to local care or those in patients with medical comorbidities, are treated with MRSA-appropriate antibiotics.

599
01:18:22,702 --> 01:18:30,052
Purulent cellulitis should be treated empirically for MRSA until culture and sensitivity results are available.

600
01:18:30,052 --> 01:18:40,327
Most CA-MRSA strains are sensitive to trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole or clindamycin (Miller, 2015; Talan, 2016).

601
01:18:40,327 --> 01:18:45,552
Rifampin rapidly develops resistance and should not be used as monotherapy.

602
01:18:45,552 --> 01:18:53,377
Linezolid, although effective against MRSA, is expensive, and little information guides its use in pregnancy.

603
01:18:53,377 --> 01:19:02,190
Although effective for MRSA infections, doxycycline, minocycline, and other tetracyclines should not be used in pregnancy.

604
01:19:02,190 --> 01:19:09,002
Vancomycin remains a first-line therapy for inpatient treatment of serious MRSA infections.

605
01:19:09,002 --> 01:19:16,740
The control and prevention of MRSA infection relies on appropriate hand hygiene and prevention of skin-to-skin contact.

606
01:19:16,740 --> 01:19:27,265
Routine screening of obstetrical patients for MRSA colonization is not recommended (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2020c.

607
01:19:27,265 --> 01:19:41,527
That said, for women with culture-proven MRSA colonization or infection, we add a single dose of vancomycin to routine beta-lactam perioperative prophylaxis for cesarean deliveries and deep perineal lacerations.

608
01:19:41,527 --> 01:19:46,327
Decolonization is not effective in the general obstetrical population .

609
01:19:46,327 --> 01:19:54,952
Breastfeeding in women colonized with MRSA is not prohibited, but optimal hygiene and attention to minor skin breaks is encouraged.

610
01:19:54,952 --> 01:19:57,502
# Listeriosis.

611
01:19:57,502 --> 01:20:09,127
Listeria monocytogenes is a facultative intracellular gram-positive bacillus that causes 100 to 200 infections requiring hospitalization each year in the United States .

612
01:20:09,127 --> 01:20:12,427
Nearly all cases are thought to be foodborne.

613
01:20:12,427 --> 01:20:21,102
Outbreaks have been caused by contaminated dairy products, fruits and vegetables, and undercooked or processed meats and deli foods.

614
01:20:21,102 --> 01:20:29,127
Invasive listerial infections are more common in pregnant women, immunocompromised patients, and the very old or young.

615
01:20:29,127 --> 01:20:36,640
For unclear reasons, infection during pregnancy is 10 to 20 times more common than in the general population .

616
01:20:36,640 --> 01:20:47,790
Although pregnancy losses linked to listeriosis are more likely to be reported, another hypothesis is that pregnant women are more susceptible because of decreased cell-mediated immunity .

617
01:20:47,790 --> 01:20:59,715
Maternal and Fetal Infection Infections with Listeria may result in noninvasive, febrile gastroenteritis, sepsis, or CNS infection after a 2- to 4-week incubation.

618
01:20:59,715 --> 01:21:09,615
Listeriosis during pregnancy may be asymptomatic or may cause a febrile illness that is confused with influenza, pyelonephritis, or meningitis.

619
01:21:09,615 --> 01:21:15,152
The diagnosis usually is not apparent until blood cultures are reported as positive.

620
01:21:15,152 --> 01:21:19,290
Occult or clinical infection also may stimulate labor.

621
01:21:19,290 --> 01:21:29,002
Transplacental bacterial invasion leads to fetal infection, which can result in stillbirth or clinical illness similar to early-onset GBS sepsis.

622
01:21:29,002 --> 01:21:38,602
A classic description of overwhelming fetal infection is that of disseminated microabscesses and granulomas known as granulomatosis infantiseptica.

623
01:21:38,602 --> 01:21:44,015
Chorioamnionitis is common, and placental macroabscesses may be seen.

624
01:21:44,015 --> 01:21:49,040
Late-onset neonatal infection can also follow exposure at delivery.

625
01:21:49,040 --> 01:22:01,377
In a review of 222 cases, infection with Listeria resulted in abortion or stillbirth in 20 percent, and neonatal sepsis developed in 68 percent of surviving newborns .

626
01:22:01,377 --> 01:22:09,965
In one prospective cohort study, 24 percent of mothers experienced fetal loss, but none after 29 weeks’ gestation .

627
01:22:09,965 --> 01:22:15,490
However, a neonatal case fatality rate of 21 percent has been reported .

628
01:22:15,490 --> 01:22:22,890
Treatment with ampicillin plus gentamicin is usually recommended because of its synergism against Listeria species .

629
01:22:22,890 --> 01:22:27,827
Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole can be given to penicillin-allergic women.

630
01:22:27,827 --> 01:22:31,202
Maternal treatment in most cases is also

631
01:22:31,372 --> 01:22:34,047
effective for fetal infection.

632
01:22:33,997 --> 01:22:36,459
No vaccine is available.

633
01:22:36,459 --> 01:22:44,309
Prevention is by washing raw vegetables, cooking all raw food, and avoiding the implicated foods listed previously .

634
01:22:44,309 --> 01:22:46,847
# Salmonellosis.

635
01:22:46,847 --> 01:22:52,472
Infections from Salmonella species continue to be a major cause of foodborne illness.

636
01:22:52,472 --> 01:23:00,959
Six serotypes, including Salmonella subtypes typhimurium and enteritidis, account for most cases in the United States.

637
01:23:00,959 --> 01:23:06,559
Nontyphoid Salmonella gastroenteritis is contracted through contaminated food.

638
01:23:06,559 --> 01:23:16,472
Symptoms that include nonbloody diarrhea, abdominal pain, fever, chills, nausea, and vomiting begin 6 to 48 hours after exposure.

639
01:23:16,472 --> 01:23:19,547
Diagnosis is made by stool studies .

640
01:23:19,547 --> 01:23:24,609
Treatment is with intravenous crystalloid solutions given for rehydration.

641
01:23:24,609 --> 01:23:33,834
Antimicrobials are not given in uncomplicated infections because they do not commonly shorten illness and may prolong the convalescent carrier state.

642
01:23:33,834 --> 01:23:40,397
If gastroenteritis is complicated by high fever or bacteremia, antimicrobials are given .

643
01:23:40,397 --> 01:23:46,222
Rare case reports have linked Salmonella enteritidis bacteremia with septic abortion .

644
01:23:46,222 --> 01:23:51,409
Typhoid fever caused by Salmonella typhi remains a global health problem.

645
01:23:51,409 --> 01:23:57,172
Infection is spread by oral ingestion of contaminated food, water, or milk.

646
01:23:57,172 --> 01:24:05,609
Although uncommon in the United States, an outbreak in Central America in 2017 involved almost 300 cases .

647
01:24:05,609 --> 01:24:23,034
Severe complications among patients requiring hospitalization include gastrointestinal bleeding—11 percent; intestinal perforation—9 percent; encephalopathy—8 percent; renal failure—6 percent; and cardiovascular collapse—7 percent .

648
01:24:23,034 --> 01:24:29,934
In pregnant women, the disease is more likely to be encountered during epidemics and in those with recent travel .

649
01:24:29,934 --> 01:24:33,109
Septic abortion is rare but reported .

650
01:24:33,109 --> 01:24:44,097
Fluroquinolones and third-generation cephalosporins are the preferred empirical treatment for enteric (typhoid) fever and invasive nontyphoidal Salmonella infection .

651
01:24:44,097 --> 01:24:52,034
Antimicrobial susceptibility testing is important to tailor the course of therapy, which typically lasts 1 to 2 weeks.

652
01:24:52,034 --> 01:24:57,972
Two licensed vaccines for prevention of typhoid fever are available in the United States .

653
01:24:57,972 --> 01:25:05,784
From limited evidence, typhoid vaccine administration to pregnant women before travel is not linked to adverse outcomes.

654
01:25:05,784 --> 01:25:08,172
# Shigellosis.

655
01:25:08,172 --> 01:25:18,697
Transmitted via the fecal–oral route, shigellosis is a common cause of diarrheal outbreaks in children attending day-care centers and in adults following international travel.

656
01:25:18,697 --> 01:25:29,434
Bacillary dysentery caused by Shigella species can range from mild diarrhea to severe dysentery with fever, bloody stools, abdominal cramping, and tenesmus.

657
01:25:29,434 --> 01:25:39,084
In immunosuppressed individuals, severe infection may lead to toxic megacolon, seizures or meningitis, or hemolytic uremic syndrome .

658
01:25:39,084 --> 01:25:44,934
Although mild infection is typically self-limited, treatment of dehydration is essential.

659
01:25:44,934 --> 01:25:51,397
Antimotility drugs are avoided, and meticulous hygiene is recommended to avoid transmission.

660
01:25:51,397 --> 01:25:58,709
Antimicrobial therapy during pregnancy includes fluoroquinolones, ceftriaxone, or azithromycin.

661
01:25:58,709 --> 01:26:05,847
Resistance is rapidly emerging, and antibiotic susceptibility testing can help guide appropriate therapy .

662
01:26:05,847 --> 01:26:11,059
Shigellosis can stimulate uterine contractions and cause preterm birth .

663
01:26:11,059 --> 01:26:13,422
# Lyme Disease.

664
01:26:13,422 --> 01:26:21,684
Caused by the spirochete Borrelia burgdorferi, Lyme disease is the most commonly reported vector-borne illness in the United States .

665
01:26:21,684 --> 01:26:26,284
Lyme borreliosis results from tick bites of the genus Ixodes.

666
01:26:26,284 --> 01:26:28,509
There are three stages .

667
01:26:28,509 --> 01:26:39,297
Early infection—stage 1—causes a distinctive local skin lesion, erythema migrans, which may be accompanied by a flu-like syndrome and regional adenopathy.

668
01:26:39,297 --> 01:26:45,272
If untreated, early disseminated infection—stage 2—follows in days to weeks.

669
01:26:45,272 --> 01:26:53,922
Multisystem involvement is frequent, but skin lesions, neuropathies, myalgia, carditis, and meningitis predominate.

670
01:26:53,922 --> 01:27:03,484
If still untreated after months, late or persistent infection—stage 3—manifests in perhaps half of patients as a migratory arthritis.

671
01:27:03,484 --> 01:27:09,972
Native immunity is acquired, and the disease enters a chronic phase in approximately 10 percent.

672
01:27:09,972 --> 01:27:18,847
Some patients remain asymptomatic, but others in the chronic phase develop various skin, joint, or neurological manifestations.

673
01:27:19,012 --> 01:27:25,374
Clinical diagnosis is important because serological and PCR testing have many pitfalls .

674
01:27:25,324 --> 01:27:36,599
IgM and IgG serological testing is recommended in early infection and is followed by a second test—either a second immunoassay or western blot—for confirmation .

675
01:27:36,599 --> 01:27:46,487
Ideally, acute and convalescent serological evaluation is completed if possible, however, rates of false-positive and -negative results are high.

676
01:27:46,487 --> 01:27:51,337
Treatment is recommended by the Infectious Diseases Society of America .

677
01:27:51,337 --> 01:27:59,962
For erythema migrans, doxycycline for 10 days or amoxicillin or cefuroxime axetil for 14 days is recommended.

678
01:27:59,962 --> 01:28:04,499
However, doxycycline is usually avoided in pregnancy.

679
01:28:04,499 --> 01:28:08,474
Azithromycin for 7 days is a second-line regimen.

680
01:28:08,474 --> 01:28:16,637
For other manifestations, a 14- to 28-day oral or intravenous course of the same first-line drugs is recommended.

681
01:28:16,637 --> 01:28:19,699
No vaccine is commercially available.

682
01:28:19,699 --> 01:28:26,849
Avoiding areas with endemic Lyme disease and improving tick control in those areas is the most effective prevention.

683
01:28:26,849 --> 01:28:34,287
Selfexamination with removal of unengorged ticks within 36 hours of attachment reduces infection risk.

684
01:28:34,287 --> 01:28:43,724
For tick bites recognized within 72 hours, a single 200-mg oral dose of doxycycline may reduce infection development .

685
01:28:43,724 --> 01:28:49,399
Several reports describe Lyme disease in pregnancy, although large series are lacking.

686
01:28:49,399 --> 01:28:57,987
Transplacental transmission has been confirmed, but no congenital effects of maternal borreliosis have been conclusively identified .

687
01:28:57,987 --> 01:29:03,874
Prompt treatment of early maternal infection should prevent most adverse pregnancy outcomes.

688
01:29:03,874 --> 01:29:15,299
In the northeast United States, between 2 and 40 percent of women with early Lyme disease may be co-infected with Babesia microti, which is another parasite transmitted by same tick.

689
01:29:15,299 --> 01:29:19,612
Babesiosis has been reported to cause congenital infection .

690
01:29:19,612 --> 01:29:22,112
# Tuberculosis.

691
01:29:22,112 --> 01:29:30,074
Diagnosis and management of tuberculosis during pregnancy is discussed in Chapter 54 (p. 965).

692
01:29:30,074 --> 01:29:32,374
Protozoal Infections.

693
01:29:32,374 --> 01:29:35,087
# Toxoplasmosis.

694
01:29:35,087 --> 01:29:41,987
The obligate intracellular parasite Toxoplasma gondii has a life cycle with two distinct stages .

695
01:29:41,987 --> 01:29:47,299
The feline stage takes place in cats—the definitive host—and their prey.

696
01:29:47,299 --> 01:29:51,187
Unsporulated oocysts are excreted in feces.

697
01:29:51,187 --> 01:30:00,187
In the nonfeline stage, tissue cysts containing bradyzoites or oocysts are ingested by the intermediate host, including humans.

698
01:30:00,187 --> 01:30:07,249
Gastric acid digests the cysts to release bradyzoites, which infect small-intestinal epithelium.

699
01:30:07,249 --> 01:30:14,599
Here, they are transformed into rapidly dividing tachyzoites, which can infect all cells within the host mammal.

700
01:30:14,599 --> 01:30:20,837
Humoral and cell-mediated immune defenses eliminate most of these, but tissue cysts develop.

701
01:30:20,837 --> 01:30:25,524
Their lifelong persistence is the chronic form of toxoplasmosis.

702
01:30:25,524 --> 01:30:36,074
Human infection is acquired by eating raw or undercooked meat infected with tissue cysts or by ingesting soil or water contaminated with oocysts from cat feces.

703
01:30:36,074 --> 01:30:44,687
Prior infection is confirmed by serological testing, and its prevalence depends on geographical locale and parasite genotype.

704
01:30:44,687 --> 01:30:54,562
The incidence of congenital toxoplasmosis in the United States has declined and now approximates 0.23 cases in 10,000 live births .

705
01:30:54,562 --> 01:31:03,712
Maternal and Fetal Infection Most acute maternal infections are subclinical and are detected only by prenatal or newborn serological screening.

706
01:31:03,712 --> 01:31:09,649
In some cases, maternal symptoms may include a flu-like illness with lymphadenopathy.

707
01:31:09,649 --> 01:31:19,424
In immunocompetent adults, initial infection confers immunity, and prepregnancy infection nearly eliminates any risk of vertical transmission.

708
01:31:19,424 --> 01:31:29,537
Infection in immunocompromised women, however, may be severe, and reactivation may cause encephalitis, chorioretinitis, or mass lesions.

709
01:31:29,537 --> 01:31:35,374
Vertical transmission can cause fetal infection and then congenital toxoplasmosis.

710
01:31:35,374 --> 01:31:41,637
The latter is a fetopathy characterized by ocular or intracranial lesions and growth delay.

711
01:31:41,637 --> 01:31:47,837
Congenital toxoplasmosis is also associated with an increased risk for preterm delivery .

712
01:31:47,837 --> 01:31:52,712
The estimated rate of vertical transmission rises with gestational age.

713
01:31:52,712 --> 01:32:02,774
In one metaanalysis, the rate was 15 percent at 13 weeks, 44 percent at 26 weeks, and 71 percent at 36 weeks .

714
01:32:02,774 --> 01:32:10,612
Although transmission risk is lower in early pregnancy, sequelae of congenital toxoplasmosis are more likely to be

715
01:32:10,780 --> 01:32:12,380
severe .

716
01:32:12,330 --> 01:32:18,242
Notably, most infected fetuses are born without obvious toxoplasmosis stigmata.

717
01:32:18,242 --> 01:32:27,167
Clinically affected neonates usually have generalized disease expressed as low birthweight, hepatosplenomegaly, jaundice, and anemia.

718
01:32:27,167 --> 01:32:34,905
Some primarily have neurological disease with intracranial calcifications, hydrocephaly, or microcephaly .

719
01:32:34,905 --> 01:32:40,130
Many eventually develop chorioretinitis and exhibit learning disabilities.

720
01:32:40,130 --> 01:32:48,967
This classic triad— chorioretinitis, intracranial calcifications, and hydrocephalus—is often accompanied by convulsions.

721
01:32:48,967 --> 01:32:54,392
Infected neonates with clinical signs are at risk for long-term complications .

722
01:32:54,392 --> 01:33:03,817
Screening and Diagnosis With IgG antibody confirmed before pregnancy, there is no risk for fetal infection in immunocompetent hosts.

723
01:33:03,817 --> 01:33:15,917
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020a) does not recommend prenatal screening for toxoplasmosis in low-prevalence areas, including the United States.

724
01:33:15,917 --> 01:33:24,380
Screening IgG serology tests should be performed in immunocompromised gravidas, including those with HIV infection.

725
01:33:24,380 --> 01:33:29,067
Pregnant women with suspected toxoplasmosis also should be tested.

726
01:33:29,067 --> 01:33:33,492
The parasite is rarely detected in tissue or body fluids.

727
01:33:33,492 --> 01:33:44,755
IgG antibodies against Toxoplasma develop within 2 to 3 weeks after infection, peak at 1 to 2 months, and usually persist for life—sometimes in high titers.

728
01:33:44,755 --> 01:33:54,355
Although IgM antibodies appear by 10 days after infection and usually become negative within 3 to 4 months, they may remain detectable for years.

729
01:33:54,355 --> 01:34:00,405
Thus, IgM antibodies are not used alone to diagnose acute toxoplasmosis.

730
01:34:00,405 --> 01:34:13,030
Best results are obtained with the Toxoplasma Serologic Profile performed at the Dr. Jack S. Remington Laboratory for Specialty Diagnostics (www.sutterhealth.org).

731
01:34:13,030 --> 01:34:17,180
Toxoplasma IgG avidity increases with time.

732
01:34:17,180 --> 01:34:24,555
Thus, if a high-avidity IgG result is found, infection in the preceding 3 to 5 months is excluded.

733
01:34:24,555 --> 01:34:33,155
Multiple tests are available that allow high-avidity results to confirm latent infection with a 100-percent positive predictive value .

734
01:34:33,155 --> 01:34:46,880
Congenital toxoplasmosis is suspected when sonography reveals findings such as hydrocephaly, intracranial or hepatic calcifications, ascites, placental thickening, hyperechoic bowel, and growth restriction.

735
01:34:46,880 --> 01:34:52,117
These are similar to abnormalities that may be seen with congenital CMV.

736
01:34:52,117 --> 01:35:05,730
If these are found incidentally, diagnostic amniocentesis with PCR testing for toxoplasma as well as cytomegalovirus DNA is recommended, along with maternal serologic testing for both infections.

737
01:35:05,730 --> 01:35:19,442
The sensitivity of PCR for detection of toxoplasma DNA varies with gestational age and is lowest before 18 weeks’ gestation (Filisetti, 2015; Yamamoto, 2017).

738
01:35:19,442 --> 01:35:25,630
Management Prenatal treatment of acute maternal toxoplasmosis is based on two regimens.

739
01:35:25,630 --> 01:35:33,080
Spiramycin may be given alone, or a pyrimethamine plus sulfonamide combination is taken with folinic acid .

740
01:35:33,080 --> 01:35:36,942
These two regimens have also been used consecutively.

741
01:35:36,942 --> 01:35:44,917
Most experts will use spiramycin in women with acute infection early in pregnancy to reduce the risk of vertical transmission.

742
01:35:44,917 --> 01:35:51,130
Because it does not cross the placenta, spiramycin may not be used to treat fetal infection.

743
01:35:51,130 --> 01:36:00,580
Pyrimethamine plus sulfadiazine with folinic acid is selected for maternal infection after 18 weeks’ gestation or if fetal infection is suspected.

744
01:36:00,580 --> 01:36:12,417
The SYROCOT study group (2007) reviewed data from 1438 treated pregnancies and found weak evidence for early treatment to reduce congenital toxoplasmosis risks.

745
01:36:12,417 --> 01:36:19,067
Treatment has been associated with a reduced rate of serious neurological sequelae and neonatal demise .

746
01:36:19,067 --> 01:36:23,717
Prevention No vaccine is available to prevent toxoplasmosis.

747
01:36:23,717 --> 01:36:50,392
Strategies to avoid infection include: (1) cooking meat to safe temperatures; (2) peeling or thoroughly washing fruits and vegetables; (3) cleaning all food preparation surfaces and utensils that have contacted raw meat or unwashed fruits and vegetables; (4) wearing gloves when changing cat litter or delegating this duty; and (5) avoiding feeding cats raw or undercooked meat and keeping cats indoors.

748
01:36:50,392 --> 01:36:58,580
Although these preventive steps are recommended, no robust data support their effectiveness (American College of Obstetricians

749
01:36:58,732 --> 01:37:04,232
and Gynecologists, 2020a; Di Mario, 2015).

750
01:37:04,182 --> 01:37:06,369
# Malaria.

751
01:37:06,369 --> 01:37:10,507
This protozoan infection remains a global health crisis.

752
01:37:10,507 --> 01:37:18,169
In 2018, it caused 260,000 deaths, and most occurred among children younger than 5 years .

753
01:37:18,169 --> 01:37:22,932
Sub-Saharan Africa and India carry the largest burden of cases.

754
01:37:22,932 --> 01:37:34,169
Malaria had been effectively eradicated in Europe and in most of North America, but some cases are imported in returning military personnel or recent immigrants from endemic areas .

755
01:37:34,169 --> 01:37:40,807
Transmitted by infected Anopheles mosquitoes, six species of Plasmodium cause human disease.

756
01:37:40,807 --> 01:37:44,119
Pregnant women have increased susceptibility .

757
01:37:44,119 --> 01:37:56,132
After infection, antibodies to the parasite surface antigen VAR2CSA help mediate placental accumulation of infected erythrocytes and lead to the harmful effects of malaria .

758
01:37:56,132 --> 01:38:03,469
Through this mechanism, some immunity accrues with parity and is termed pregnancy-specific antimalarial immunity.

759
01:38:03,469 --> 01:38:07,469
Ironically, malaria treatment dampens this immunity.

760
01:38:07,469 --> 01:38:15,869
Maternal and Fetal Infection Clinical manifestations are fever, chills, and flu-like symptoms, which may occur episodically.

761
01:38:15,869 --> 01:38:19,157
Symptoms are less severe with recurrences.

762
01:38:19,157 --> 01:38:28,557
Malaria may be associated with anemia and jaundice, and infections with Plasmodium falciparum may cause kidney failure, coma, and death.

763
01:38:28,557 --> 01:38:36,369
That said, many otherwise healthy but infected adults in endemic areas are asymptomatic because of partial immunity.

764
01:38:36,369 --> 01:38:43,069
Pregnant women, although often asymptomatic, are said to be more likely to develop traditional symptoms.

765
01:38:43,069 --> 01:38:52,269
Malarial infections in pregnancy—either symptomatic or asymptomatic— are associated with higher rates of perinatal morbidity and mortality .

766
01:38:52,269 --> 01:38:58,707
Adverse outcomes include stillbirth, preterm birth, low birthweight, and maternal anemia.

767
01:38:58,707 --> 01:39:02,782
These correlate with high levels of placental parasitemia.

768
01:39:02,782 --> 01:39:11,894
The maternal anemia and low birthweight are documented most frequently (Machado Filho, 2014; McClure, 2013).

769
01:39:11,894 --> 01:39:19,944
Approximately 16 percent of low-birthweight neonates born to women in Africa are attributable to maternal malaria infection .

770
01:39:19,944 --> 01:39:28,394
Effects stem from parasitized erythrocytes, monocytes, and macrophages that accumulate in the vascular areas of the placenta .

771
01:39:28,394 --> 01:39:34,719
Infections with P falciparum are the worst, and early infection raises the risk for abortion.

772
01:39:34,719 --> 01:39:41,057
The incidence of malaria increases significantly in the latter two trimesters and postpartum .

773
01:39:41,057 --> 01:39:48,019
Despite this, congenital malaria develops in <5 percent of neonates born to infected mothers.

774
01:39:48,019 --> 01:39:50,832
Figure 67-7.

775
01:39:50,832 --> 01:39:54,044
Photomicrograph of placental malaria.

776
01:39:54,044 --> 01:39:59,432
Multiple infected erythrocytes are seen, and two are identified (arrows).

777
01:39:59,432 --> 01:40:03,982
Cross section of nearby chorionic villus is shown (arrowhead).

778
01:40:03,982 --> 01:40:12,769
Diagnosis and Management Identification of parasites by microscopic evaluation of a blood smear remains the diagnostic gold standard.

779
01:40:12,769 --> 01:40:18,907
In women with low parasite densities, however, the sensitivity of microscopy is poor.

780
01:40:18,907 --> 01:40:26,057
Malaria-specific antigens are now being used for rapid testing, which has become the diagnostic standard .

781
01:40:26,057 --> 01:40:32,519
For treatment, the most frequently used antimalarial drugs are not contraindicated in pregnancy.

782
01:40:32,519 --> 01:40:44,107
The World Health Organization recommends that all infected patients living in or traveling from endemic areas be treated with an artemisinin-based regimen for uncomplicated falciparum malaria .

783
01:40:44,107 --> 01:40:51,444
The CDC (2020c) recommends that initial treatment be determined by where the infection was acquired.

784
01:40:51,444 --> 01:41:04,369
Pregnant women diagnosed with uncomplicated malaria caused by P vivax, P malariae, P ovale, and chloroquine-sensitive P falciparum should be treated with hydroxychloroquine or chloroquine .

785
01:41:04,369 --> 01:41:15,507
For women infected with multidrug-resistant P falciparum, the first-line agent for gravidas in the second and third trimesters is a drug containing both artemether and lumefantrine.

786
01:41:15,507 --> 01:41:28,582
The PREGACT Study Group (2016) compared four artemisinin-based drugs in 3428 pregnant women with falciparum malaria and reported no serious maternal or perinatal adverse effects.

787
01:41:28,582 --> 01:41:32,932
Chloroquine-resistant P vivax should be treated with mefloquine.

788
01:41:32,932 --> 01:41:41,069
Chloroquine-sensitive P vivax or P ovale should be treated with chloroquine throughout pregnancy and then primaquine postpartum.

789
01:41:41,069 --> 01:41:44,794
Resistance to all the antimalarial drugs has

790
01:41:44,956 --> 01:41:50,193
been reported, including the recently added artemisinin-based compounds.

791
01:41:50,143 --> 01:42:02,743
Treatment regimens for uncomplicated and severe malarial infections in pregnancy are detailed at: www.cdc.gov/malaria/diagnosis_treatment.

792
01:42:02,743 --> 01:42:11,593
The CDC also maintains a malaria hotline for treatment recommendations (855856-4713).

793
01:42:11,593 --> 01:42:20,568
Prevention and Chemoprophylaxis Malaria control and prevention relies on chemoprophylaxis when traveling to or living in endemic areas.

794
01:42:20,568 --> 01:42:23,518
Vector control also is important.

795
01:42:23,518 --> 01:42:32,281
Insecticidetreated netting, pyrethroid insecticides, and DEET-based insect repellent lower malarial rates in endemic areas.

796
01:42:32,281 --> 01:42:35,318
These are well tolerated in pregnancy.

797
01:42:35,318 --> 01:42:39,756
If travel is necessary, chemoprophylaxis is recommended.

798
01:42:39,756 --> 01:42:45,768
Chloroquine or hydroxychloroquine prophylaxis is safe and well tolerated in pregnancy.

799
01:42:45,768 --> 01:42:57,918
One evaluation compared prophylaxis during pregnancy with either sulfadoxine-pyrimethamine or dihydroartemisininpiperaquine and found the latter to be more effective in preventing placental infection .

800
01:42:57,918 --> 01:43:06,068
Atovaquone/proguanil use is approved for gravidas, however, primaquine and doxycycline are contraindicated .

801
01:43:06,068 --> 01:43:18,543
The latest chemoprophylaxis regimens for pregnancy can be obtained from the CDC Travelers’ Health website at: www.cdc.gov/malaria/travelers/.

802
01:43:18,543 --> 01:43:28,793
The CDC also publishes Health Information for International Travel (The Yellow Book) at: www.cdc.gov/yellowbook.

803
01:43:28,793 --> 01:43:40,843
For women living in endemic areas, intermittent preventive treatment with sulfadoxine-pyrimethamine is superior to intermittent screening plus indicated treatment, although resistance is a growing concern .

804
01:43:40,843 --> 01:43:43,293
# Amebiasis.

805
01:43:43,293 --> 01:43:51,193
Approximately 10 percent of the world population is infected with Entamoeba histolytica, and most are asymptomatic .

806
01:43:51,193 --> 01:43:56,506
Amebic dysentery, however, may take a fulminant course during pregnancy.

807
01:43:56,506 --> 01:44:00,843
Symptoms are fever, abdominal pain, and bloody stools.

808
01:44:00,843 --> 01:44:05,106
Prognosis is worse if complicated by a hepatic abscess.

809
01:44:05,106 --> 01:44:11,306
Identifying E histolytica cysts or trophozoites within a stool sample is diagnostic.

810
01:44:11,306 --> 01:44:14,806
Therapy is similar to that for nonpregnant woman.

811
01:44:14,806 --> 01:44:21,156
For amebic colitis and invasive disease, tinidazole may be preferred over metronidazole, .

812
01:44:21,156 --> 01:44:23,506
Mycotic Infections.

813
01:44:23,506 --> 01:44:34,868
Disseminated fungal infection—usually pneumonitis—during pregnancy is uncommon with coccidiomycosis, blastomycosis, cryptococcosis, or histoplasmosis.

814
01:44:34,868 --> 01:44:41,331
Their identification and management are considered in Chapter 54 (p. 965).

815
01:44:41,331 --> 01:44:47,418
Vulvovaginal candidiasis is described in Chapter 68 (p. 1218).

816
01:44:47,418 --> 01:44:50,393
Travel Precautions During Pregnancy.

817
01:44:50,393 --> 01:44:57,031
Pregnant travelers face general medical, obstetrical, and potentially hazardous destination risks.

818
01:44:57,031 --> 01:45:00,443
Several sources provide travel information .

819
01:45:00,443 --> 01:45:17,493
The International Federation for Tropical Medicine has comprehensive information available at www.iftm-hp.org, and the International Society of Travel Medicine publishes information at www.istm.org.

820
01:45:17,493 --> 01:45:24,606
Also, The Yellow Book from the CDC has extensive travel information regarding pregnancy and breastfeeding.

821
01:45:24,606 --> 01:45:26,581
Bioterrorism.

822
01:45:26,581 --> 01:45:35,718
The concept of bioterrorism involves the deliberate release of bacteria, viruses, or other infectious agents to cause illness or death.

823
01:45:35,718 --> 01:45:42,331
These natural agents are often altered to raise their infectivity or their resistance to medical therapy.

824
01:45:42,331 --> 01:45:54,243
Health-care providers should be alert for significant increases in the number of persons with febrile illnesses accompanied by respiratory symptoms or with rashes not easily associated with common illnesses.

825
01:45:54,243 --> 01:46:01,568
Clinicians are urged to contact their state health department or the CDC for current information and recommendations.

826
01:46:01,568 --> 01:46:12,006
In their Committee Opinion, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019b) has addressed disaster preparedness for obstetricians.

827
01:46:12,006 --> 01:46:14,393
# Smallpox.

828
01:46:14,393 --> 01:46:19,543
The variola virus causes smallpox and is considered a serious weapon.

829
01:46:19,543 --> 01:46:25,618
The virus is highly transmissible and carries an overall 30-percent case fatality rate.

830
01:46:25,618 --> 01:46:35,193
The last case of smallpox in the United States was reported in 1949, and worldwide it was reported in Somalia in 1977.

831
01:46:35,193 --> 01:46:43,418
Nishiura (2006) has reviewed the severe perinatal and maternal morbidity and mortality caused by smallpox.

832
01:46:43,418 --> 01:46:48,968
The case fatality rate of smallpox is 61 percent if the pregnant woman is

833
01:46:49,132 --> 01:46:51,044
unvaccinated.

834
01:46:50,994 --> 01:47:00,069
Rates of stillbirth, abortion, preterm delivery, and neonatal demise rise significantly in pregnancies complicated by this infection.

835
01:47:00,069 --> 01:47:03,744
The smallpox vaccine is made with live virus.

836
01:47:03,744 --> 01:47:08,632
Thus, pregnancy should be delayed for 4 weeks after vaccination.

837
01:47:08,632 --> 01:47:10,919
# Anthrax.

838
01:47:10,919 --> 01:47:16,282
Bacillus anthracis is a gram-positive, spore-forming, aerobic bacterium.

839
01:47:16,282 --> 01:47:23,282
It can cause three main types of clinical anthrax: inhalational, cutaneous, and gastrointestinal .

840
01:47:23,282 --> 01:47:30,069
Anthrax meningitis may accompany any of the clinical anthrax forms and is fatal in most cases.

841
01:47:30,069 --> 01:47:34,569
When inhaled, anthrax spores are deposited in the alveoli.

842
01:47:34,569 --> 01:47:39,719
They are engulfed by macrophages and germinate in mediastinal lymph nodes.

843
01:47:39,719 --> 01:47:45,307
The incubational period is usually less than 1 week but may be as long as 2 months.

844
01:47:45,307 --> 01:47:54,394
Within 1 to 5 days of symptom onset, the second stage is heralded by the abrupt onset of severe respiratory distress and high fevers.

845
01:47:54,394 --> 01:47:59,457
Mediastinitis and hemorrhagic thoracic lymphadenitis are common.

846
01:47:59,457 --> 01:48:03,069
Chest radiographs show a widened mediastinum.

847
01:48:03,069 --> 01:48:10,569
Case-fatality rates with inhalational anthrax are high, even with aggressive antibiotic and supportive therapy.

848
01:48:10,569 --> 01:48:13,819
# Other Bioterrorism Agents.

849
01:48:13,819 --> 01:48:28,919
Other category A bioterrorism agents include Francisella tularensis— tularemia, Clostridium botulinum—botulism, Yersinia pestis—plague, and Ebola, Marburg, Lassa, or Machupo viruses—hemorrhagic fever.

850
01:48:28,919 --> 01:48:39,882
The guidelines for these biological agents are evolving and are detailed at the CDC Bioterrorism website: emergency.cdc.gov/bioterrorism.

